export const extendedGuides = {
  "raiden-shogun": {
  "guide": "Raiden Shogun is often misunderstood as just a burst DPS, but her real value comes from being an energy cycle engine for the entire team. When building her, the single most important stat is Energy Recharge - not just because her burst needs energy, but because her A4 passive converts every point of ER above 100% into Electro DMG Bonus. At 270% ER (achievable with Engulfing Lightning and ER sands), she gains 68% Electro DMG from this passive alone, plus another 60%+ from the Emblem of Severed Fate set bonus.\n\nIn combat, the key to maximizing Raiden is rotation discipline. The standard setup is Raiden E -> support bursts -> Raiden Q -> N3C combo. Her skill (E) provides coordinated attacks that boost burst damage for all party members. Never use her burst first - always apply buffs from Bennett, Sara, or Kazuha before switching to Raiden.\n\nRaiden National (Raiden + Xiangling + Xingqiu + Bennett) is the most consistent team in the game because everyone batteries everyone else. Xiangling and Xingqiu get energy from Raiden, and Raiden gets resolve stacks from their bursts. This creates a seamless energy loop that requires minimal ER on supports.\n\nWhere she struggles: Raiden underperforms in quick-swap teams that dont rely on burst rotations, or against enemies that move out of her burst range frequently. Against multi-wave content, her energy refund mechanic becomes less valuable because waves reset rotation cycles."
},
  "hu-tao": {
  "guide": "Hu Taos damage comes from a fundamental tradeoff: survival versus damage. Her E skill converts HP to ATK, and her A4 passive gives 33% Pyro DMG Bonus when HP is below 50%. This means the optimal playstyle constantly walks the line between maximum damage and dying.\n\nAt C0, stamina management is the most important skill to learn. Each charged attack consumes stamina, and Hu Tao needs enough left to dodge. The solution is jump-canceling - pressing jump immediately after a charged attack lands cancels the recovery animation and saves stamina. The optimal C0 combo is N1C-Jump (Normal Attack -> Charged Attack -> Jump Cancel), repeated 8-9 times per E duration.\n\nAt C1, stamina management becomes trivial and her damage ceiling increases significantly because you can dash-cancel instead of jump-cancel, which is faster and allows more charged attacks per rotation. This is widely considered the most impactful C1 in the game.\n\nThe double Hydro core (Yelan + Xingqiu) solves Hu Taos biggest weakness: inconsistent Vaporize uptime. With two Hydro units providing off-field application, every charged attack triggers Vaporize. Without this setup, some attacks will apply Pyro instead of reacting, losing 50% of potential damage.\n\nWhere she fails: Hu Tao requires a shielder (preferably Zhongli) for endgame content. She is also difficult to use on mobile devices where jump-cancels are harder to execute."
},
  "neuvillette": {
  "guide": "Neuvillette is unique among Genshin DPS characters because his damage does not depend on elemental reactions at all. His charged attack beam scales purely on HP (40,000+ is the target), ignoring ATK completely. This means his damage is consistent regardless of enemy elements - he performs equally well against Hydro slimes and Pyro regisvines.\n\nHis key mechanic is the Sourcewater Droplet system. Using his E skill creates 6 droplets, and his Q creates 6 more. Collecting 3 droplets reduces his charged attack charge time to nearly instant. The optimal rotation is: E -> Q -> CA (with 3 droplets) -> wait for E cooldown -> collect remaining droplets -> CA again.\n\nThe most common mistake is not managing droplet collection properly. Each CA needs exactly 3 droplets for instant charge. Picking up more than 3 wastes battery potential for the next CA cycle.\n\nNeuvillettes best team is with Furina - his self-healing from CA triggers Furinas fanfare stacks, and Furinas buff dramatically increases his damage. The rotation with this team requires dropping his CA early if the team needs healing, even if it means slightly less personal damage.\n\nWhere he struggles: Neuvillette is extremely vulnerable to interruption. Without a shield (Zhongli) or interrupt resistance (Baizhu), getting hit during the CA beam wastes significant DPS. His linear beam AoE is also wasted against spread-out enemies."
},
  "nahida": {
  "guide": "Nahida fundamentally changed Genshins meta by enabling Dendro reactions to their full potential. Before her release, Dendro teams were functional but clunky. With Nahida, they became some of the strongest team archetypes in the game.\n\nHer biggest value isnt her personal damage - its her off-field Dendro application. Her E skill marks enemies (up to 8) and triggers coordinated Dendro damage whenever reactions occur. This works with any reaction - Hyperbloom, Burgeon, Spread, Aggravate, Burning, Bloom. The mark persists even when Nahida is off-field, making her a true set-and-forget support.\n\nThe A4 passive is what makes her truly broken: she shares 25% of her Elemental Mastery to the on-field character. At 1000 EM, thats 250 free EM to your main DPS or trigger unit. This is why shes mandatory in Hyperbloom teams - she boosts Kuki or Raidens EM significantly, which directly increases Hyperbloom damage.\n\nBuilding Nahida is straightforward: as much EM as possible. The EM breakpoint is 800-1000. Below 800, she falls off noticeably. Above 1000, diminishing returns make other stats more valuable. If shes in a Spread team, a hybrid build (EM/Dendro/Crit) deals more personal damage.\n\nWhere she struggles: Nahida is extremely squishy and has limited range on her mark. Against enemies spread across the battlefield, she needs another Dendro unit to maintain coverage. Her personal damage in non-Dendro teams is zero."
},
  "zhongli": {
  "guide": "Zhonglis value proposition is simple: he makes the game easier. His jade shield (hold E) has the highest shield strength in the game and provides 20% universal RES shred to everything within range. There is no enemy in the game that can break a properly built Zhongli shield before it expires.\n\nThe RES shred is his hidden power - 20% RES reduction is equivalent to roughly a 10-15% total damage increase for the team, depending on the enemys base resistance. This applies to ALL elements, unlike VV shred which only works for swirled elements. This makes him valuable in any team composition.\n\nBuilding Zhongli is straightforward: maximize HP. With 50,000+ HP, his shield has over 25,000 absorption. A hybrid build (HP/Geo/Crit) sacrifices shield strength for burst damage. The burst-damage build is viable if you have C2 (which provides shields for co-op), but for pure support, full HP is better.\n\nThe controversy around Zhongli centers on opportunity cost. In teams with strong healers (Kokomi, Baizhu, Bennett), his shield is often overkill - you would get more total damage from another buffer like Kazuha or Yelan. Hes best used in teams where interruption resistance is critical.\n\nWhere he falls short: Zhongli provides no energy generation and very low personal damage (unless built hybrid). In abyss speedruns, he is often replaced by offensive supports who contribute more to clear times."
},
  "kazuha": {
  "guide": "Kazuha provides two independent damage amplification mechanics that stack multiplicatively: Viridescent Venerer 4pc (40% RES reduction to swirled element) and his A4 passive (0.04% Elemental DMG Bonus per EM point). At 1000 EM, thats 40% Elemental DMG Bonus. Together, these provide roughly a 60-70% total damage increase for the team.\n\nHis double-swirl mechanic is what separates good Kazuha players from great ones. By applying an element to himself (via Bennett Q, for example), Kazuha can absorb a different element with his burst, swirling both elements and providing VV shred for both. This is critical in reaction teams like International (Childe + Xiangling), where both Hydro and Pyro need shredding.\n\nBuilding Kazuha is simple: Elemental Mastery in every slot. EM/EM/EM with EM substats. Freedom-Sworn is his best weapon for the team ATK buff, but Iron Sting (craftable) works fine. The EM target is 800-1000. Beyond 1000, the conversion rate diminishes.\n\nThe Sucrose debate is worth understanding. C6 Sucrose with Thrilling Tales can match or exceed Kazuhas buff contribution in reaction-heavy teams (especially Vaporize and Melt). Kazuha wins in mono-element teams and freeze teams where consistent double-swirl isnt necessary.\n\nWhere he underperforms: Kazuha struggles against bosses that cant be pulled by his skill. His CC is wasted against heavy enemies. In those scenarios, his value comes purely from his damage buffs, which are still strong but relatively less impactful."
},
  "yelan": {
  "guide": "Yelans strength comes from two unique mechanics: ramping burst damage and team damage amplification. Her burst gains 3.5% more damage per second over 15 seconds, reaching a 50% damage increase at full duration. Her A4 passive increases the active characters damage by 1% for every 10 seconds of burst duration (also up to 50%).\n\nThis means Yelans value INCREASES over the course of combat - longer rotations disproportionately favor her. This is why she pairs so well with Hu Tao (long on-field time) and is less impactful in quick-swap teams.\n\nBuilding Yelan requires balancing HP, ER, and CRIT. Unlike most characters, Yelan gains nothing from ATK - her damage scales purely on HP. The ER requirement depends on her weapon: with Favonius Warbow, she needs 160-180% ER as solo Hydro. With Elegy for the End, 180-200%. With Aqua Simulacra, she needs 140-160% if paired with another Hydro unit.\n\nHer E skill is one of the best mobility tools in the game, allowing her to cover large distances quickly. This is useful both in combat (repositioning) and overworld traversal. The skill also generates a significant amount of energy particles.\n\nWhere she struggles: As solo Hydro, Yelans ER requirements are punishing. Her damage also falls off significantly without proper investment in HP artifacts, which are harder to farm than traditional ATK-based artifacts. She also cannot replace Xingqiu in teams that need his damage reduction and interrupt resistance."
},
  "alhaitham": {
  "guide": "Alhaithams complex mirror mechanic is the key to his high damage ceiling. His E skill generates 1 mirror, plunge attacks generate 1, and his Q generates 3. Each mirror increases his attack speed and converts his attacks to Dendro damage. Three mirrors provide roughly 90% more damage than one mirror.\n\nThe optimal rotation requires managing mirror count carefully. Start with 0 mirrors: Q (generates 3) -> N4D x3 (consumes 1 mirror every 4 seconds) -> when down to 1 mirror, use E and plunge to refresh to 3 -> continue attacking. This gives you approximately 12 seconds of 3-mirror uptime per rotation.\n\nAlhaitham works in two team archetypes: Spread and Quickbloom. Spread teams maximize his personal damage with Electro off-fielders (Fischl, Yae Miko). Quickbloom teams sacrifice some personal damage for Hyperbloom procs (with Kuki or Raiden). Both are viable, but Spread has a higher ceiling with proper investment.\n\nHis best stat spread is different from most DPS characters: he wants EM more than ATK. EM Sands is often better than ATK Sands, especially in Spread teams where EM increases both his personal damage and reaction damage.\n\nWhere he struggles: Alhaithams rotation is punishing if interrupted. Getting knocked back during the mirror-optimization phase can ruin the entire rotation. He also requires field time that conflicts with other on-field units. Without Nahida on the team, his personal damage drops significantly."
},
  "kokomi": {
  "guide": "Kokomi's value is not her damage - it is her ability to hold a slot that heals, applies Hydro off-field, and carries a supportive artifact set at the same time. Her jellyfish (E) ticks Hydro every 2 seconds for 12 seconds at C0, and her burst refreshes the jellyfish duration, which means a properly played Kokomi has near-permanent Hydro uptime without standing still.\n\nThe single most important build rule: never build CRIT on Kokomi. Her passive converts CRIT Rate into healing bonus and sets her CRIT Rate to a negative value, so CRIT stats are literally dead weight. Every roll should go into HP%, Energy Recharge, or Healing Bonus.\n\nKokomi fits three very different team archetypes. In Freeze teams (Ayaka + Shenhe + Kazuha + Kokomi) she is the off-field Hydro aura that keeps enemies frozen. In Nilou Bloom she is the on-field driver who triggers most of the Bloom cores while healing the self-damage from Bountiful Cores. In Sukokomon (Kokomi + Fischl + Xiangling + Sucrose) she drives Overloaded and Electro-Charged procs.\n\nWhere she struggles: Kokomi has essentially zero personal damage, so she only earns her slot when the team needs healing and Hydro at the same time. In teams that already have a strong healer or that do not need Hydro aura, a damage-oriented Hydro unit is usually better."
},
  "clorinde": {
  "guide": "Clorinde is the first on-field Electro DPS built entirely around the Bond of Life mechanic. Casting her skill enters a duelist stance and grants Bond of Life; while she has Bond of Life, her normal attacks become piercing shots that deal Electro damage and consume part of the bond. Any healing she receives while the bond is active is converted into more Bond of Life (up to a cap), which in turn becomes more damage - this is why healers that continuously tick (like Baizhu or Yaoyao) feel so good with her.\n\nHer rotation is simple but punishing if interrupted: E (skill) -> N4D x3 -> Q (burst resets the skill and deals AoE Electro). Dash-canceling after the 4th hit keeps her mobile and re-applies Bond of Life through coordinated healing.\n\nClorinde's strongest archetype is Aggravate, where Fischl provides off-field Electro and a Dendro unit enables the reaction. Overload variants with Chevreuse are also strong because they buff ATK and shred Electro RES. Her worst archetype is mono-Electro with no reaction support, where her raw multipliers are only average for a modern DPS.\n\nWhere she struggles: Clorinde is interruption-sensitive during her normal attack strings, so a shielder or a healer with poise is strongly recommended. She also wants consistent Electro teammates - without Fischl or another off-field Electro unit, her Aggravate uptime drops noticeably."
},
  "nilou": {
  "guide": "Nilou does not deal meaningful personal damage, and that is fine - she is the enabler of one of the strongest AoE archetypes in the game. Her passive replaces normal Bloom cores with Bountiful Cores that detonate almost instantly and deal damage in a larger radius. Critically, this passive only works when the party contains Hydro and Dendro characters exclusively, so team building around her is a hard constraint rather than a preference.\n\nHer contribution scales with nothing but HP: every 1,000 HP above 30,000 increases Bountiful Core damage, capping at roughly 74,444 HP for the maximum bonus. This means her entire build is HP% main stats with HP% substats, and her weapons are judged almost entirely on how much HP they give (Key of Khaj-Nisut is far ahead of everything else).\n\nThe standard Nilou Bloom rotation is counterintuitive: she opens with E x3 (Sword Dance -> Whirling Steps -> Luminous Illusion) to set up her Hydro ring, then swaps out and lets the Dendro and Hydro appliers generate cores. The on-field driver should be the Hydro healer (Kokomi or Barbara) so Bloom self-damage is healed, with Nahida providing the Dendro aura.\n\nWhere she struggles: Nilou is completely inflexible. Any Pyro, Electro, Cryo, or Anemo teammate disables her core passive, and against single-target bosses Bountiful Core teams are much weaker than Hyperbloom or traditional carries."
},
  "shenhe": {
  "guide": "Shenhe is a pure Cryo amplifier, and her kit is easier to misunderstand than almost any other support. Her skill grants Icy Quills to the whole party; each quill adds a flat damage bonus to Cryo damage instances based on her own ATK (roughly 15% of her ATK per quill). The quills are consumed by damage instances, not by characters, which is the key to using her correctly.\n\nThis has two practical consequences. First, Shenhe's value scales with her total ATK, not with CRIT - stacking ATK% in every slot is correct, and CRIT stats on her are nearly worthless. Second, her quills are consumed extremely fast in AoE, so her buff is strongest in single-target scenarios and in teams where the Cryo DPS deals a few very large hits (Ayaka's burst, Ganyu's charged shots).\n\nShenhe's own damage is negligible, and her burst (which shreds Cryo RES and deals AoE Cryo) is her secondary contribution. Her C1 gives an extra skill charge (double the quills), and her C2 adds a team-wide Cryo DMG bonus - both are large upgrades, but C0 already functions.\n\nWhere she struggles: Shenhe only fits Cryo teams. Outside of Freeze and Cryo Melt compositions she contributes almost nothing, and in heavy AoE content her quill stacks evaporate before the rotation ends."
},
  "kuki-shinobu": {
  "guide": "Kuki Shinobu is the best Hyperbloom trigger in the game, and the reason is simple: her skill follows the active character and applies Electro from off-field while she heals. That combination - off-field Electro plus healing - is exactly what a Hyperbloom team needs in its fourth slot, and no other character provides both as reliably.\n\nHyperbloom damage scales only with two things: the trigger's Elemental Mastery and the trigger's character level. ATK, CRIT, and DMG bonuses do nothing. This means Kuki's optimal build is unusual: level her to 90 (non-negotiable - reaction damage scales hard with level), then stack full EM in every artifact slot (EM Sands, EM Goblet, EM Circlet) and give her an EM weapon.\n\nThe tradeoff is healing. Kuki's healing scales with HP, so a full EM build noticeably reduces her output. In practice this is still enough healing for Hyperbloom because Bloom self-damage is small compared to Abyss incoming damage, but if your team is dying, an HP% Sands or Circlet is an acceptable compromise.\n\nWhere she struggles: Kuki's skill costs her own HP to cast, and at full EM she is squishy. She also has no crowd control and no damage outside of reaction triggers, so in teams that do not use Hyperbloom or Overload she is a much weaker pick."
},
  "furina": {
  "guide": "Furina is the strongest generic damage amplifier in the game, and her kit is built on one tradeoff: she drains the whole party's HP to build Fanfare stacks, then converts those stacks into a team-wide damage bonus of up to 75% at C0. This is why she effectively requires a team-wide healer - without one you get a fraction of the buff and a party that is permanently at low HP.\n\nHer skill has two stances. Ousia (default) summons three companions that deal off-field Hydro damage and drain party HP - this is the damage stance you use in almost every team. Pneuma summons a single healer that reverses the drain when your team already has enough sustain. Switching stances is free, so the correct play is Ousia for damage and Pneuma only when you need healing mid-fight.\n\nFurina's damage scales with HP, but her burst buff scales with nothing you can build - it depends only on how many Fanfare stacks you generate. That makes her unusually forgiving: her personal build (HP and CRIT) and her support value are almost independent.\n\nWhere she struggles: any team that cannot heal the whole party loses a large part of her value. Single-target healers like Bennett or Kuki are much worse with her than team-wide healers like Baizhu, Kokomi, Jean, or Charlotte."
},
  "bennett": {
  "guide": "Bennett's burst does two things at once: it heals whoever stands in the circle and it grants a massive ATK buff to the active character. The buff is calculated from Bennett's base ATK only - that is his character base ATK plus his weapon's base ATK. Artifact ATK% does nothing for the buff, which is the single most misunderstood thing about building him.\n\nTwo practical rules follow from that. First, give him the highest base ATK sword you own (Aquila Favonia, Mistsplitter, or any 5-star stat stick) rather than an ER sword, as long as you can still reach his Energy Recharge needs through artifacts. Second, level his burst talent as high as possible, because the buff scales with talent level.\n\nBennett is also the best battery in the game for Pyro teams: his short skill cooldown generates a large number of Pyro particles, which is exactly why Xiangling is functionally tied to him. The downside of his kit is the circle - mobile enemies and spread-out fights punish teams that rely on standing still.\n\nWhere he struggles: his C6 infuses the circle with Pyro, which overrides Physical melee and some elemental infusions, so many players deliberately leave C6 unactivated. He also provides nothing outside the circle, so teams that roam around the battlefield lose his value."
},
  "xingqiu": {
  "guide": "Xingqiu's burst is the strongest off-field Hydro application in the game. His rainswords fire alongside the active character's normal attacks, applying Hydro fast enough to let Hu Tao Vaporize every charged attack or to fuel Hyperbloom core generation. On top of that, his rainswords grant damage reduction and interruption resistance, which is why so many carries feel unplayable without him.\n\nThe catch is energy. His burst costs 80 energy with a 20-second cooldown, so at C0 he needs roughly 180-200% Energy Recharge as the only Hydro unit. Sacrificial Sword solves this by letting him cast his skill twice; at C6 his needs drop to around 140-160%. Without Sacrificial Sword, an ER Sands plus an ER weapon is mandatory.\n\nOne of the most useful details about Xingqiu is that his burst rainswords only trigger on normal attacks - charged attacks do not trigger them. This matters for carries like Hu Tao, whose damage comes from charged attacks but who still weaves a normal attack before each charge.\n\nWhere he struggles: he has no healing beyond minor damage reduction and no crowd control, and against a single boss with no adjacent targets his sword orbitals lose a portion of their damage."
},
  "xiangling": {
  "guide": "Xiangling is free, she is a 4-star, and she has been in the meta since launch for one reason: her burst snapshots. Guoba (skill) is forgettable, but Pyronado (burst) records her stats at the moment of casting - so if Bennett's ATK buff, Noblesse Oblige, and Viridescent Venerer shred are all active when she casts, Pyronado keeps those buffs for its entire 14-second duration even after they expire.\n\nThis single mechanic is why the Xiangling plus Bennett pairing is the backbone of National teams. Cast her burst outside Bennett's circle and you lose roughly a third of her damage.\n\nHer second defining trait is her energy cost. Pyronado costs 80 energy with no particle generation while it is active, which is why she needs 200%+ Energy Recharge and a dedicated battery (almost always Bennett). The Catch R5 - a completely free weapon obtained from fishing - is her best 4-star option because it gives ER, burst damage, and CRIT Rate together.\n\nWhere she struggles: Xiangling is unusable without a battery and heavily punished by bad rotation order. She also has no defensive utility, and her Pyronado orbits at a fixed radius, so mobile enemies can walk out of it."
},
  "arlecchino": {
  "guide": "Arlecchino is the cleanest implementation of the Bond of Life mechanic released so far. Her skill marks enemies; using a charged attack collects the marks and grants her Bond of Life, which scales her normal attack damage. The more Bond of Life she has, the harder she hits - and unlike Hu Tao, she does not need to sit at low HP to get there.\n\nThe important restriction: while her Bond of Life is active, she cannot be healed by anyone except her own burst. This makes traditional healers nearly useless with her and pushes her toward shielders (Zhongli, Layla) or self-sustain via her burst. Bennett's healing is also wasted on her, even though his ATK buff still works.\n\nHer best teams are Vaporize (with Xingqiu or Yelan) and Overload (with Chevreuse). Both archetypes multiply her already large Pyro multipliers. Mono-Pyro with Bennett and Kazuha is a comfortable third option.\n\nWhere she struggles: Arlecchino is fragile by design - she cannot be healed mid-rotation, so a mistimed dodge is far more punishing than on Hu Tao. She also wants a shielder in most endgame content, which costs you a buffer slot."
},
  "ayaka": {
  "guide": "Ayaka is the Freeze benchmark. Her burst has the highest AoE damage uptime in the game, her sprint infuses Cryo and lets her traverse water, and her alternate sprint gives her free Cryo application on demand. In any content where enemies can be frozen she is close to unbeatable.\n\nHer biggest build advantage is that Freeze teams get CRIT Rate for free: Blizzard Strayer 4pc grants up to 40% CRIT Rate against frozen or Cryo-affected enemies, and Cryo resonance adds another 15%. That means an Ayaka with 30-40% CRIT Rate from artifacts is already near the cap, so every remaining roll can go into CRIT DMG and ATK%.\n\nHer classic combo is dash -> E -> N2C x2 -> Q -> N2C spam, and the timing matters: apply Hydro before her burst so enemies stay frozen while the burst ticks through them.\n\nWhere she struggles: Ayaka is completely dependent on Freeze. Against bosses that cannot be frozen she loses her CRIT Rate sources, her crowd control, and a large part of her damage - in those fights most players simply use a different carry."
},
  "ganyu": "Ganyu is the most flexible Cryo unit in the game because she has two completely separate playstyles. As a Freeze carry she runs Blizzard Strayer and uses her burst as a massive off-field Cryo aura while another unit drives. As a Melt carry she stands on field, charges Frostflake Arrows, and reverse-Melts them off Xiangling's Pyronado for the single largest per-hit numbers a Cryo unit can produce.\n\nThe Frostflake Arrow has two charge levels, and the second charge blooms into an AoE Cryo hit - that bloom is what makes her Melt damage so high, because both the arrow and the bloom can react. Her burst is a persistent AoE Cryo field with no ICD issues for aura purposes, which is why Freeze teams value it even when Ganyu is not the driver.\n\nWhere she struggles: charge shots are slow and interruption ends them immediately, so she needs a shielder (Zhongli or Layla) to function in Melt. On mobile, aiming charged shots is genuinely difficult, and against mobile bosses her Melt arrows simply miss.",
  "xiao": "Xiao trades his own HP for the highest plunge multipliers in the game. During his burst his jumps cost HP, his attack range expands, and his plunge damage scales off ATK - which makes him one of the few carries that wants pure ATK stacking with no reaction dependency at all.\n\nThat simplicity is also his weakness: Xiao has no reaction to multiply, so his damage ceiling is decided almost entirely by his supports. Faruzan is not optional - she provides Anemo RES shred, Anemo DMG bonus, and particle generation, and at C6 she even lets his burst crit-consistently battery itself. Add Furina or Xianyun and Xiao jumps tier lists; without them he feels noticeably weaker than modern carries.\n\nWhere he struggles: Xiao drains his own HP during his burst, so he needs a healer or shielder in every team, and his plunge pattern is predictable and interruption-prone. He also has no off-field presence whatsoever - when his burst ends, he contributes nothing.",
  "tartaglia": "Childe's entire value is one thing: he applies Hydro faster than any other character in the game from melee range. That single property is what makes International (Childe + Xiangling + Bennett + Kazuha) work, because every Pyronado hit Vaporizes instead of every other hit.\n\nHis melee stance (E) is a stance change with a scaling cooldown - the longer you stay in melee, the longer the cooldown before you can use it again. This is the core skill of playing Childe: stay in melee just long enough to let Xiangling's Pyronado run its full duration, then swap out and let the cooldown tick while your supports do their work. Overstaying in melee is the single most common Childe mistake.\n\nWhere he struggles: Childe has no defensive utility in melee stance and takes more damage while in it. His ranged burst (used at range) has a much shorter cooldown, so ranged burst into melee swap is the correct pattern rather than melee into melee.",
  "arataki-itto": "Itto is the rare carry that scales on DEF instead of ATK. His burst converts his DEF into attack damage and gives him Geo infusion, and his Ushi (skill) both generates energy and taunts enemies. Because his entire kit scales on one stat, his artifacts are unusually easy to please: DEF Sands, Geo DMG Goblet, CRIT Circlet, then CRIT and DEF% substats.\n\nHis real constraint is team building. Itto wants Gorou, who provides DEF buffs, Geo DMG bonus, and Geo particles, and he wants a third Geo unit for resonance and crystallize shields. That means Itto teams are effectively mono-Geo, which gives up reactions entirely in exchange for very stable, shield-protected damage.\n\nWhere he struggles: mono-Geo has no reaction multiplier, so Itto's ceiling is fixed by his own stats. He also has no off-field damage, and against enemies with high Geo resistance or elemental shields, Geo teams have no way to break those shields quickly.",
  "yoimiya": "Yoimiya is the easiest carry in the game to play well. Press her skill and her normal attacks become Pyro-infused with auto-targeting, so she never misses and never needs to aim. For players who struggle with animation cancels or positioning, she converts almost all of her theoretical damage into real damage.\n\nThe tradeoff is AoE. Yoimiya is strictly single-target - her arrows hit one enemy at a time, so she is strong against bosses and weak against grouped mobs. She is also interruption-sensitive: getting hit mid-attack string cancels the rest of it, which is why Zhongli or a shielder is effectively mandatory in her best teams.\n\nHer two viable archetypes are Vaporize (with Xingqiu or Yelan) and Overload (with Fischl, Beidou, or Chevreuse). Vaporize has the higher single-target ceiling; Overload gives her the AoE she otherwise lacks.\n\nWhere she struggles: no AoE, no crowd control, and heavy reliance on a shielder. In Abyss halves with many small enemies she is one of the weakest carry choices available.",
  "yae-miko": "Yae Miko is the strongest off-field Electro damage dealer in the game. Her skill plants three Sesshou Sakura turrets that continuously fire Electro at enemies, and her burst consumes and refreshes them for a burst of AoE damage. Once the turrets are placed she contributes damage while standing completely off-field.\n\nHer kit has two real costs. First, placing three turrets takes field time (three skill casts), so she wants to come in, set up, and leave. Second, her burst costs 90 energy, which is the highest in the game - she needs 130-150% ER or an Electro battery to use it every rotation, and many players simply skip it in Aggravate teams where her turret damage is the point.\n\nYae's best archetype is Aggravate, where Dendro turns her Electro application into reaction damage. She also works in Taser and as a Hyperbloom enabler, though her turrets target randomly which makes her a poor Hyperbloom trigger specifically.\n\nWhere she struggles: she provides no buffs, no healing, and no shielding - she is a pure damage slot. Against single bosses with no adds, her turret targeting is less efficient than Fischl's Oz.",
  "cyno": "Cyno is a long-field-time Electro carry. His burst extends his stance, converts his attacks to Electro, and refreshes its own duration through his Endorse mechanic, which means he wants to stay on field for 12-18 seconds at a time. That is both his identity and his biggest problem.\n\nBecause he occupies the field for so long, his teams must be built entirely around supporting him while he is out. Quickbloom (Cyno + Nahida + Xingqiu + Baizhu) is his strongest archetype: he triggers both Aggravate and Hyperbloom damage simultaneously, and Baizhu's interrupt resistance prevents his long attack strings from being cancelled.\n\nHis stat line is also unusual - he wants EM as much as ATK, because both Aggravate and Hyperbloom scale with it. An EM Sands is often correct on him, which is rare for an on-field carry.\n\nWhere he struggles: Cyno has almost no team flexibility. His long field time conflicts with any other carry, and without interrupt resistance his entire rotation collapses when he gets hit.",
  "mavuika": "Mavuika is the highest-ceiling Pyro carry currently available. Her skill puts her on a motorcycle that deals continuous Pyro damage and works as a traversal tool, while her burst is one of the largest single hits in the game. Both scale off her Fighting Spirit, a resource she builds when nearby characters consume Nightsoul.\n\nThis is the key to her team building: Mavuika wants Natlan teammates (Citlali, Xilonen, Ororon, Iansan) because their Nightsoul consumption is what charges her Fighting Spirit fastest. In a team with no other Natlan unit she still functions, but her burst comes up much less often.\n\nHer strongest archetype by far is Melt, because her burst has an enormous single-instance multiplier that Cryo aura turns into a massive reaction hit. Citlali is the ideal partner - she applies Cryo off-field, shreds Pyro RES, and charges Fighting Spirit at the same time.\n\nWhere she struggles: her best team is expensive (Mavuika + Citlali + Xilonen + Bennett), and her damage pattern is heavily front-loaded into her burst, so missing the Melt setup wastes most of her value.",
  "kinich": "Kinich is a Dendro claymore carry built around one mechanic: his skill fires a grappling hook, and while attached he circles the enemy and fires cannon shots. All of his meaningful damage is skill damage, which means his normal attacks barely matter and his entire build should serve that one window.\n\nThe hook also changes how he positions. Instead of standing in front of an enemy, he orbits it — which makes him naturally good against large single targets and awkward against scattered mobs, because re-attaching the hook costs time.\n\nKinich's best archetype is Burning, where a Pyro teammate keeps a burning aura on the enemy and his Dendro shots convert that into consistent bonus damage. Burgeon variants also work, though they require more precise Hydro timing.\n\nWhere he struggles: he needs a Pyro teammate to reach his ceiling, his damage is awkward against groups, and missing the grapple wastes several seconds of his rotation.",
  "xilonen": "Xilonen is the most flexible defensive support released in years, because she compresses three jobs into one slot: she shreds Elemental RES, she heals the whole party, and she enables Natlan carries through Nightsoul synergy.\n\nHer skill works by matching the element of her teammates. The more Pyro, Hydro, Electro, or Cryo characters you bring, the more elements she can shred RES for — which means she scales with team diversity rather than forcing one specific composition. Her healing scales with DEF, and it is strong enough to sustain Furina teams on its own.\n\nBecause her value comes from DEF rather than CRIT or EM, her build is simple and cheap: stack DEF in every main stat and let her kit do the rest. That also makes her unusually easy to gear compared to other 5-star supports.\n\nWhere she struggles: her RES shred depends on your team's element spread, so mono-element teams get less from her than mixed teams do. She also provides no crowd control and very little personal damage.",
  "citlali": "Citlali is the best Melt enabler in the game. She brings three things a Pyro carry wants at once: an off-field Cryo aura to Melt against, Pyro RES shred, and a shield to protect the carry while they attack.\n\nThat combination is why she is best-in-slot for Mavuika and a strong option for Arlecchino and Gaming teams. Before Citlali, Melt teams had to choose between Cryo application (Kaeya, Rosaria) and defensive utility — she removes that tradeoff entirely.\n\nHer kit scales with Elemental Mastery, which is unusual for a shielder: more EM means a stronger shield, better reaction damage, and more value from her supporting artifact set. This makes her one of the few supports where EM is the correct stat in every slot.\n\nWhere she struggles: her shield is weaker than Zhongli's, her Cryo application depends on her burst being ready, and outside Pyro Melt teams much of her value goes unused.",
  "mualani": "Mualani is a Hydro catalyst carry who fights from a shark-mounted stance. Her damage comes from marking enemies while surfing and then biting them for large Hydro hits, with the final bite scaling on her max HP. That makes her a HP-scaling burst carry rather than a sustained one.\n\nHer entire kit is built around Vaporize. Without a Pyro aura to react against, she loses roughly half of her potential damage — so every serious Mualani team needs reliable off-field Pyro (Xiangling is the standard answer, and Mavuika is the premium one).\n\nBecause she wants Obsidian Codex, which grants up to 40% CRIT Rate, her artifact requirements are unusually forgiving: build around 60% CRIT Rate and pour the rest into CRIT DMG and HP%.\n\nWhere she struggles: she has no self-healing, she is interruption-sensitive while surfing, and against scattered enemies her marking and bite pattern loses most of its value.",
  "chasca": "Chasca is an Anemo bow carry who fights from the air and converts her teammates' elements into her own damage. The core rule is simple: the more Pyro, Hydro, Electro, and Cryo characters you bring, the more elemental bullets she loads, and the more damage her shots deal.\n\nThis makes her one of the most team-flexible carries in the game, because she benefits from elemental diversity rather than demanding a specific reaction setup. Flying also means she ignores most ground-based mechanics, which makes her comfortable in content where positioning is awkward.\n\nHer tradeoff is focus. Because her damage is split across several elements, she rarely reaches the peak damage of a carry built around one reaction — she trades ceiling for flexibility and comfort.\n\nWhere she struggles: she wants a team with multiple different elements, which conflicts with mono-element or resonance-based teams, and her damage is spread out rather than concentrated.",
  "venti": "Venti has the strongest crowd control in the game, and when it works nothing else comes close: his burst pulls almost every small enemy into one point and continuously re-groups them while dealing damage. He also refunds energy to the team for the element absorbed by his burst, which lets teammates run much lower Energy Recharge.\n\nThat is also his limitation. Modern endgame content is full of large enemies and bosses that resist pulling, and against those enemies Venti's burst does far less — his crowd control becomes a damage-only ability, and other Anemo supports simply out-buff him.\n\nThe practical way to use Venti is as a content check: if the chamber is full of small pullable enemies, he is the best unit you can bring. If it is a boss chamber, bring Kazuha or Sucrose instead.\n\nWhere he struggles: he contributes almost nothing against bosses that cannot be pulled, and his burst can whiff entirely against enemies that move during the animation.",
  "wanderer": "Wanderer is an Anemo catalyst carry who fights while floating, which lets him ignore most ground hazards and hit enemies that are hard to reach. His damage comes from fast normal attacks during his skill window, and he has one of the smoothest attack animations among catalyst users.\n\nHis entire viability rests on Faruzan. She provides Anemo RES shred, Anemo DMG bonus, and energy, and without her his damage drops dramatically — there is no realistic Wanderer team that skips her.\n\nHis second requirement is interruption resistance. Getting hit while floating ends his damage window and drops him, so a shielder (Zhongli or Layla) is effectively mandatory in difficult content.\n\nWhere he struggles: he is the most interruption-sensitive carry in the game, he is locked to Faruzan, and he provides nothing when he is not on field.",
  "navia": "Navia is a Geo claymore carry built around front-loaded burst damage. Her skill consumes Crystallize shards, and each shard massively increases the damage of her shot — with three shards, a single skill press is one of the largest instant hits in the game.\n\nThis design makes her much more flexible than Itto. Because Crystallize only requires any elemental aura plus Geo, she works in teams with Pyro, Hydro, Electro, or Cryo supports, rather than being locked into mono-Geo.\n\nHer rotation is also short: apply elements, collect shards, fire the skill, swap out. That low field time makes her easy to fit alongside another carry.\n\nWhere she struggles: her damage is concentrated in a few big hits, so missing or mistiming the skill wastes most of her rotation. She also needs teammates that reliably create shards, and her sustained damage is below Itto's.",
  "baizhu": "Baizhu is the best pure defensive support for reaction teams. His burst creates a shield that refreshes periodically while healing the active character, and his skill heals the whole party — which is exactly what Furina teams need to build Fanfare stacks.\n\nHe also provides interruption resistance, which quietly makes him best-in-slot for carries with long attack strings: Cyno, Neuvillette, and Wanderer all perform noticeably better with him.\n\nHis Dendro application is weak compared to Nahida, so he is not a replacement for her — he is the sustain option you add when the team already has enough damage and needs to survive.\n\nWhere he struggles: he contributes almost no damage, his Dendro application is too slow to drive reaction teams on its own, and his burst has a high energy cost that needs real ER investment.",
  "skirk": "Skirk is a Cryo sword carry whose damage comes from her skill stance rather than her burst, which makes her fundamentally different from Ayaka. She does not need enemies to be frozen to deal her damage — she simply enters her stance and attacks.\n\nThat single difference is why she is more reliable in modern content. Ayaka's value collapses against bosses that cannot be frozen; Skirk keeps her full damage output regardless, which makes her usable in far more chambers.\n\nHer teams still favour Hydro and Cryo supports for resonance and reaction value, and Furina remains an ideal partner because Skirk's own HP mechanics help build Fanfare stacks quickly.\n\nWhere she struggles: as a newer unit her optimal rotations are still being refined by the community, and her best-in-slot weapon options are limited, which makes weapon choice more impactful than for older carries.",
  "keqing": "Keqing was the first Electro carry in the game and spent years being considered weak — until Dendro arrived. Aggravate changed everything, because it adds flat damage to her very fast Electro hits, and her kit is built around hitting often rather than hitting hard.\n\nHer rotation is the fastest in the game: skill, then a charged attack or a second skill cast to trigger her thunderclap, then swap. Because her field time is short, she fits into quick-swap teams where other carries would waste buff uptime.\n\nHer best team is Aggravate: Keqing + Fischl + Nahida (or Baizhu) + Kazuha/Sucrose. Fischl is the key partner, because her A4 passive triggers additional Electro damage whenever the active character triggers an Electro reaction — which happens constantly with Keqing.\n\nWhere she struggles: her raw multipliers are lower than modern Electro carries, so she depends entirely on Aggravate to compete. She also wants 200-300 EM alongside normal CRIT stats, which makes her harder to build than a pure ATK carry.",
  "diluc": "Diluc is the original Pyro carry, and his kit is still perfectly functional: three sequential skill casts with a short cooldown, a burst that infuses Pyro, and Crimson Witch of Flames as a set that was practically designed for him. He is easy to play, self-sufficient, and needs no constellations.\n\nThe honest problem is numbers. Modern Pyro carries (Hu Tao, Arlecchino, Mavuika, Lyney) simply have higher multipliers and better mechanics, so Diluc needs significantly more investment to reach the same clear times. He is not unusable — he is just outclassed.\n\nHis best team is still the classic Vaporize setup: Diluc + Xingqiu + Bennett + Sucrose (or Kazuha). Because his skill has three charges, he can weave them between Xingqiu's rainsword triggers for consistent Vaporize uptime.\n\nWhere he struggles: his damage ceiling is fixed by old multipliers, and he has no unique mechanic (no Bond of Life, no Nightsoul, no HP scaling) to multiply his output. Players who like him can absolutely clear content; players chasing efficiency will get more from newer carries.",
  "mona": "Mona is a burst amplifier disguised as a Hydro support. Her burst applies the Omen debuff, which increases the damage enemies take for a few seconds — and if the enemy is frozen while Omen is active, the duration is extended. That interaction is why she is a staple of one-shot showcase teams and Freeze compositions.\n\nHer skill provides a taunt that applies Hydro, which is useful for crowd control but applies far less Hydro than Xingqiu or Kokomi. This is the key limitation: Mona is not a Hydro aura bot, she is a damage window enabler.\n\nBecause her value comes from her burst, she wants Emblem of Severed Fate plus enough Energy Recharge (around 200%) to burst every rotation. Thrilling Tales of Dragon Slayers is an excellent weapon on her for the ATK buff it passes to the next character.\n\nWhere she struggles: her Hydro application is too weak to drive reaction teams on her own, and if you mistime her burst the Omen window expires before your carry starts attacking — which wastes her entire contribution.",
  "eula": "Eula is the only serious Physical carry in the game, and her design is built around one big moment: her burst stacks Grimheart stacks as she attacks, then detonates for enormous Physical damage. Everything else in her kit exists to feed that detonation.\n\nPhysical damage has a specific weakness — it is reduced by enemy Physical resistance, which many bosses and elite enemies have. That is why Superconduct (Electro plus Cryo, reducing Physical RES by 40%) is mandatory for her, which in turn means she always needs an Electro teammate. Raiden is the ideal choice because she also solves Eula's energy problem.\n\nHer build is straightforward: Pale Flame 4pc, Physical DMG Goblet, ATK Sands, CRIT Circlet. Energy Recharge matters more on her than on most carries, because her burst costs 80 energy and the entire team exists to set it up.\n\nWhere she struggles: she has the longest setup of any carry, her burst can miss entirely if the enemy moves or phases, and Physical teams cannot take advantage of reaction multipliers the way elemental teams can.",
  "klee": "Klee is a catalyst Pyro carry who throws bombs, and her gameplay is defined by one mechanic: her charged attacks cost stamina but deal far more damage than her normals, so optimal play means animation-canceling every charged attack with a jump or a dash.\n\nThat cancel requirement is the reason she feels bad to many players. At high skill she is competent; without cancels her damage drops sharply, and her short range plus slow animations make her uncomfortable in endgame content with aggressive enemies.\n\nHer best team is Mono-Pyro or Vaporize: Klee + Kazuha + Bennett + Xiangling. Because she applies Pyro extremely quickly, she also works as the driver in Overload and even Burgeon-adjacent compositions.\n\nWhere she struggles: her multipliers are below modern Pyro carries, she requires animation cancels to be competitive, and her attacks scatter small enemies — which then have to be chased down.",
  "jean": "Jean is the most complete healer-support in the game: her burst heals the entire party instantly and creates a field that continuously heals the active character, while her skill provides crowd control and Viridescent Venerer shred. She compresses healing and Anemo support into one slot.\n\nHer most interesting interaction is Sunfire: combining her burst with Bennett's burst applies Pyro to your own active character, which Jean's field then swirls repeatedly for continuous Pyro damage. It is a niche but powerful pattern in specific teams.\n\nBecause her healing scales with ATK, her build is unusual for a support — she wants ATK% rather than HP%. That also means she contributes more personal damage than most healers.\n\nWhere she struggles: her skill launches small enemies upward, which can scatter groups instead of grouping them, and her crowd control is weaker than Kazuha's or Venti's. In teams that need EM or tight grouping, Sucrose is usually the better Anemo support.",
  "albedo": "Albedo is a pure off-field Geo sub-DPS: place his Solar Isotoma, and it deals Geo damage automatically while also creating a platform that lifts your active character. He requires almost no field time and no energy management beyond his modest burst cost.\n\nHis entire kit scales on DEF, which makes him one of the cheapest characters to build well — DEF is a common substat, and his best weapon (Cinnabar Spindle) was a free event weapon. Husk of Opulent Dreams is his set, and it stacks DEF and Geo DMG over time.\n\nThe main weakness of his kit is fragility: the Isotoma flower can be destroyed by bosses, and against enemies that move around it simply stops contributing. His damage is also steady rather than high, so he rarely decides a fight on his own.\n\nWhere he struggles: he has been largely outclassed by Chiori, who does the same job with higher damage and no placement issues. Albedo is still perfectly usable, but he is no longer the best choice for the role.",
  "kamisato-ayato": "Ayato is the most flexible Hydro carry in the game. His skill enters a stance where his normal attacks become fast Hydro slashes, which means he can drive Vaporize, Electro-Charged, Bloom, or Freeze depending entirely on his teammates. No other Hydro carry covers that many archetypes.\n\nThat flexibility is his selling point and his limitation. He is very good at many things and best-in-slot at none — Neuvillette out-damages him, Childe enables faster Vaporize, and Nilou is stronger in Bloom. What Ayato offers is a comfortable carry who fits into whatever team you already have built.\n\nHis burst is also valuable: it applies Hydro in a large AoE off-field, so even when he leaves the field he keeps enabling reactions. Heart of Depth and Echoes of an Offering are both solid sets for him.\n\nWhere he struggles: his damage ceiling is mid-tier among Hydro carries, and his value depends on having teammates that can use his Hydro application. In a vacuum he is a comfortable, reliable carry rather than a dominant one.",
  "fischl": "Fischl is the best 4-star sub-DPS in the game, and her value comes from two things: Oz applies Electro off-field with the most reliable single-target uptime available, and her A4 passive deals additional Electro damage every time the active character triggers an Electro-related reaction.\n\nThat A4 passive is the reason she appears in Aggravate, Taser, Overload, and Hyperbloom-adjacent teams. It scales with the whole team's reaction activity rather than with Fischl's own field time, so she contributes damage while doing almost nothing.\n\nHer kit is also unusual in that her burst refreshes Oz rather than being a separate damage source. At C6, Oz stays on field for the full duration and attacks alongside the active character, which is one of the strongest 4-star constellations in the game.\n\nWhere she struggles: her damage is strictly single-target, so in large mob pulls she contributes far less than Beidou or Yae Miko. Oz can also target the wrong enemy when several are grouped together.",
  "sucrose": "Sucrose is the best 4-star Anemo support, and she does three things that matter: she holds Viridescent Venerer for 40% RES shred, she shares a large portion of her Elemental Mastery with the team, and she can hold Thrilling Tales of Dragon Slayers to hand a big ATK buff to your carry.\n\nThat combination makes her a genuine budget replacement for Kazuha. In reaction-heavy teams — Vaporize, Melt, Hyperbloom — a C6 Sucrose can actually match or exceed Kazuha's contribution, because EM share is worth more than Elemental DMG Bonus when reactions are doing the damage.\n\nShe also generates a good number of particles and has access to Sacrificial Fragments, which lets her cast her skill twice per rotation for more energy and more swirls.\n\nWhere she struggles: her crowd control is weaker and less reliable than Kazuha's or Venti's, and her buffs require real EM investment before they matter — a low-investment Sucrose contributes very little beyond VV shred.",
  "beidou": "Beidou is the AoE counterpart to Fischl. Her burst creates a lightning arc that jumps between enemies, which makes her one of the strongest sub-DPS units in multi-target content — and one of the weakest in single-target content.\n\nHer skill is a counter mechanic: timing it to absorb an incoming hit deals significantly more damage and generates more particles. Learning the counter timing is the difference between a mediocre Beidou and a strong one, and it is the main reason she feels awkward to new players.\n\nHer burst costs 80 energy, which is the real constraint. She needs roughly 180% Energy Recharge or a second Electro unit to battery her, and Emblem of Severed Fate turns that ER requirement into damage.\n\nWhere she struggles: she is dependent on her burst, punished by high ER requirements, and her value drops sharply against a single boss where the lightning arc has nothing to chain to.",
  "faruzan": "Faruzan is the dedicated Anemo support, and she is the reason Anemo carries are viable at all. Her burst provides Anemo RES shred and an Anemo DMG Bonus, and her skill generates particles while applying a debuff that further reduces Anemo resistance.\n\nThe problem with Faruzan is energy. Her burst is expensive relative to her particle generation, so at C0 she needs around 250% Energy Recharge — which forces her onto Favonius Warbow and ER artifacts and leaves little room for damage.\n\nHer C6 fixes almost all of it: it lets her burst generate additional particles and adds CRIT DMG for Anemo damage. That is why the community treats C6 Faruzan as a different character from C0 Faruzan.\n\nWhere she struggles: outside Anemo hypercarry teams (Xiao, Wanderer, Heizou) she contributes nothing, and before C6 her ER requirements are genuinely uncomfortable to build around.",
  "gorou": "Gorou is the Geo equivalent of Faruzan: a dedicated buffer who only works in one archetype. His skill and burst grant DEF and Geo DMG Bonus to the team, with the size of the buff scaling on how many Geo characters you bring.\n\nThat design makes him mandatory for Itto and valuable for Noelle, but it also means he is almost useless outside mono-Geo teams. There is no reason to put Gorou in a team with two or fewer Geo units.\n\nHis build is simple: Energy Recharge until his burst is comfortable (around 200%), then DEF% and CRIT for the small amount of personal damage he contributes. Favonius Warbow is usually his best weapon because the particles help the whole team.\n\nWhere he struggles: zero flexibility. He provides no healing (unless C4), no shielding beyond crystallize, and his buffs do nothing for non-Geo characters.",
  "layla": "Layla is the strongest 4-star shielder in the game and the cheapest way to give a carry interruption resistance. Her shield scales with HP, has near-100% uptime, and she applies Cryo off-field while it is active — which also makes her useful in Freeze teams.\n\nHer real competition is Diona, and the choice comes down to what your team needs: Layla has a stronger shield and better Cryo application uptime, while Diona brings healing and energy generation. Teams that already have a healer prefer Layla; teams that need sustain prefer Diona.\n\nBecause she wants Tenacity of the Millelith, she also gives the team an ATK buff on top of the shield, which is more value than most defensive units provide.\n\nWhere she struggles: she has no healing at all, her personal damage is negligible, and her shield is still meaningfully weaker than Zhongli's.",
  "tighnari": "Tighnari is a quick-swap Dendro carry built around one trick: after using his skill, his next three charged shots fire almost instantly and home in on enemies. That window lets him unload his damage in about four seconds and then leave the field.\n\nThis makes him fundamentally different from Alhaitham. Where Alhaitham wants long on-field windows, Tighnari wants to come in, fire, and swap — which is much easier to fit around other units' cooldowns.\n\nHis best archetype is Spread, where Quicken adds flat damage to his Dendro hits. Electro supports like Fischl and Yae Miko are therefore part of his core rather than optional additions.\n\nWhere he struggles: he is a single-target-focused unit in an element that has strong AoE options, his burst costs 40 energy but provides little damage, and his damage ceiling is below Alhaitham's in sustained fights.",
  "rosaria": "Rosaria is the most flexible budget Cryo support. Her burst applies Cryo off-field in an AoE, and her passive shares up to 15% of her CRIT Rate with the party — which is a genuinely strong buff for carries that are still building their artifacts.\n\nShe works in three different roles: Cryo applicator for Freeze teams, reverse-Melt enabler with Bennett and Xiangling, and a Physical team support where her CRIT share and Cryo application both matter.\n\nHer build is straightforward — Emblem of Severed Fate or Noblesse Oblige, ATK or ER Sands, Cryo DMG Goblet, CRIT Circlet — and Favonius Lance makes her a useful battery as well.\n\nWhere she struggles: her personal damage is modest, her skill has a long cooldown, and at high investment she is outclassed by Shenhe in Cryo teams (though Shenhe costs a 5-star and Rosaria does not).",
  "chevreuse": "Chevreuse is the enabler who turned Overload from a novelty into a real archetype. Her passive shreds Pyro and Electro RES and buffs ATK — but only while every character in the party is Pyro or Electro. That restriction is severe, and it is also what makes her valuable: within a pure Pyro/Electro team her buffs are among the strongest in the game.\n\nBecause Overload knocks enemies back, her teams work best against enemies that are not easily displaced, or paired with units that can pull them back. Raiden, Clorinde, Arlecchino, and Yoimiya are the usual partners.\n\nHer build is HP-focused, because both her healing and her buff scale with HP. Noblesse Oblige or Song of Days Past are her sets, and The Catch works surprisingly well on her.\n\nWhere she struggles: the team-building restriction is absolute. Adding one Anemo, Geo, Dendro, Hydro, or Cryo unit disables her core passive entirely.",
  "emilie": "Emilie is an off-field Dendro sub-DPS built specifically for Burning teams. Her skill places a scent-collection turret that deals Dendro damage over time, and its damage increases significantly while enemies are affected by Burning.\n\nThat makes her the opposite of a generalist: she is weak in Hyperbloom or Spread teams, and strong in exactly one archetype. If you are running a Burning team (typically with Pyro carries like Arlecchino, Mavuika, or Kinich), she is the best sub-DPS available.\n\nHer build is a normal ATK and CRIT carry build with Unfinished Reverie as her set, and she needs very little field time — cast the skill, maybe the burst, then swap.\n\nWhere she struggles: she has almost no value outside Burning teams, and her damage depends on the Burning aura being maintained, which requires a reliable Pyro teammate."
,
  "wriothesley": {
    "guide": "Wriothesley is the only Cryo catalyst on-field DPS, and the mechanic that defines him is the HP line he draws at 50%. His skill (Darkgold Wolfbite) puts him into the Chill of Punishment state, and while his HP stays above half his charged attacks gain the Recipe of Survival bonus - a large, ATK-scaled damage increase. Drop below half and that bonus is replaced by the Recipe of Resurrection, which instead heals him on the same charged attacks. Learning to ride the 50% line is the whole build.\n\nHis burst (Black-Clad Brute) keeps the momentum going: it deals AoE Cryo, taunts enemies with the Icefang Rush, and heals him based on his Max HP. Because his skill and burst both move HP around, he self-triggers HP-fluctuation gear - which is exactly why 4pc Marechaussee Hunter is his best set even though it was designed for Furina teams. Blizzard Strayer is the alternative if you are not running a shielder or Furina.\n\nFor stats, run ATK% Sands, Cryo DMG Goblet, CRIT Circlet. His energy needs are low for a 5-star carry - around 110-130% - because his burst is only 60 cost and he generates his own particles. Weapons follow the stat-stick logic: Cashflow Supervisor is his signature and gives the HP ceiling he wants, while Tidal Shadow, Ballad of the Fjords and even Lost Prayer are all strong.\n\nHis best teams lean on Cryo resonance and a shielder so he can charge-attack safely. Furina is an outstanding partner because she both feeds his HP swings and benefits from them, with Shenhe, Kazuha or Xingqiu/Yelan filling the rest for Freeze. Where he struggles: against enemies that punish standing still, and in content where you cannot control his HP, his damage drops sharply."
  },
  "lyney": {
    "guide": "Lyney is a Fontaine Pyro bow DPS whose entire kit is built around one prop: the Grin-Malkin Hat. His skill (Boum-ooom!) summons that hat, which taunts enemies and - crucially - enables his enhanced charged attacks. With the hat on the field, his charged attack becomes a Flare-Up shot that ignores charge time and deals AoE Pyro, so the rotation is skill, then two or three immediate charged attacks, then swap.\n\nHis passive, Triangle of Enchantment, is why he wants the hat and a second Pyro teammate. Each Pyro construct or hat on the field grants him a stacking Pyro DMG Bonus, reaching a meaningful bump at C0 and far more at higher constellations. His burst (Wondrous Trick: Miracle Parade) drops a big AoE Pyro and respawns a hat, which is the easiest way to keep the bonus stacked.\n\nBuild him like a normal Pyro carry: ATK% Sands, Pyro DMG Goblet, CRIT Circlet, with Energy Recharge landing around 110-130% so his 60-cost burst stays available. 4pc Marechausse Hunter is strong because his passive periodically drains his HP, and 4pc Crimson Witch is the choice for Vaporize. His signature, The First Great Magic, is tailor-made for him; Aqua Simulacra, Thundering Pulse and Polar Star all work.\n\nHis core teams are mono-Pyro (Bennett + Xiangling for Vaporize, plus Kazuha or Zhongli) or a Furina variant. Where he struggles: he is a single-target bow user with weak AoE, and he is awkward to play without a shielder because aiming charged attacks leaves him exposed."
  },
  "chiori": {
    "guide": "Chiori is a Geo sword sub-DPS who fights through summoned dolls rather than her own body. Her skill (Fluttering Hasode) has two forms: tap places a doll at her location, while hold sends a doll out, has it strike, and - if you stand on the created platform - lets her jump to a second doll. Both dolls deal off-field Geo damage, and that is where almost all of her value sits.\n\nThe single most important rule for Chiori is the Geo teammate requirement. Her passive grants an extra doll whenever another Geo character is in the party, taking her from one off-field attacker to two. Without a second Geo unit she loses roughly half her damage, so she is almost never worth running in a flex slot - pair her with Zhongli, Albedo, Itto, Navia or Noelle.\n\nHer burst (Hiyoriboshi no Michi) adds a third burst of AoE Geo and refreshes the field. For stats, ATK% Sands, Geo DMG Goblet and CRIT Circlet are standard, with modest Energy Recharge because her 80-cost burst is a nice-to-have rather than her core. 4pc Golden Troupe maximizes her skill damage since the dolls count as Elemental Skill hits; 2pc Geo plus 2pc ATK is the budget option. Her signature Uraku Misugiri is far and away her best weapon, with Haran Geppaku Futsu, Mistsplitter Reforged and Wolf-Fang as alternatives.\n\nWhere she struggles: she is locked to Geo teams, her dolls can miss fast or flying enemies, and she offers no healing or shielding of her own."
  },
  "dehya": {
    "guide": "Dehya is a Pyro claymore whose kit is honest about its flaws: she is a defensive enabler first and a damage dealer a distant second. Her skill (Molten Inferno) creates the Fiery Sanctum field, which redirects a portion of incoming damage away from the active character and reduces it. Within the field, her coordinated Leonine Bite attacks trigger when the active character's normal attacks land, giving modest off-field Pyro application.\n\nThe catch is well known: once you cast her skill, the active character is locked inside her field and cannot be swapped out normally, which disrupts most team rotations. That is why her strongest home is as a Burgeon trigger for Nilou teams, where the field's Pyro application feedsBloom cores without needing her on field for long, or as a budget Pyro unit in casual content.\n\nHer burst (Leonine Bite) deals AoE Pyro and summons a larger field, so build her for HP and Energy Recharge if you want burst uptime, or ATK for personal damage. 4pc Emblem of Severed Fate is the standard for burst-focused builds, while Tenacity of the Millelith turns her into a support. Weapons are flexible: Wolf's Gravestone, Beacon of the Reed Sea, or the craftable Mailed Flower all perform.\n\nWhere she struggles: her damage per screen is low, her field disrupts rotations, and she is generally outclassed by every other Pyro unit in dedicated DPS roles. Use her for comfort and Burgeon, not for ceilings."
  },
  "kaeya": {
    "guide": "Kaeya is the free starter Cryo unit who quietly does two jobs better than most 5-stars: he batteries the team and he applies Cryo off-field. His skill (Frostgnaw) hits twice and generates three Cryo particles on press, which makes him one of the few units who can feed his own burst reliably. His burst (Glacial Waltz) sends out orbiting icicles for 12 seconds of continuous off-field Cryo - long enough to cover most rotations.\n\nBecause his value is application and energy rather than raw damage, his build is flexible. 4pc Blizzard Strayer turns him into a respectable Freeze DPS, while 4pc Emblem of Severed Fate or Noblesse Oblige support the team. Aim for enough Energy Recharge to keep his 60-cost burst up - roughly 130-160% depending on how many other Cryo units you run - and use Favonius Sword to hand those particles to a carry like Ayaka or Ganyu.\n\nHis best teams are Freeze cores (Ayaka, Ganyu, Mona), Superconduct setups for Physical carries (Eula, Razor, Freminet), and as a universal Cryo battery. He also fits Melt and Taser comps where off-field Cryo is needed.\n\nWhere he struggles: his personal damage falls off at high investment compared to dedicated 5-star Cryo units, and his burst icicles can miss fast-moving or flying enemies. As a battery and enabler, though, he remains one of the best value units in the game."
  },
  "barbara": {
    "guide": "Barbara is the quintessential Hydro healer, and her job is keeping the team alive while enabling Hydro reactions. Her skill (Let the Show Begin) has two modes: the default heals the active character over time and applies Hydro to nearby enemies every few seconds, while the alternate creates a stronger field that also heals the whole party. Her burst (Shining Miracle) is a full-party heal scaled off her Max HP.\n\nThe famous downside is the self-application: while her skill field is active, Barbara and anyone standing in it become Hydro-imbued, which can trigger unwanted Freeze or self-Swirl. The standard fix is to step out of the field during combat, or to accept it as a Freeze enabler in Barbara + Cryo teams.\n\nBuild her for HP and Healing Bonus, with a circlet that prioritizes healing. 2pc Maiden Beloved plus 2pc Tenacity of the Millelith is the default, while Ocean-Hued Clam converts her healing into damage. Her signature-class support weapon is Thrilling Tales of Dragon Slayers, which gives a massive ATK buff to the next character swapped in; Prototype Amber and Hakushin Ring are alternatives.\n\nWhere she struggles: she has no shielding, her Hydro self-application can be a liability, and her damage is negligible. She is a pure comfort pick - excellent for exploration and casual content, less ideal in tight endgame rotations where a shielder or off-field Hydro is preferred."
  },
  "ningguang": {
    "guide": "Ningguang is the Geo catalyst queen whose entire kit is about the Jade Screen. Her skill (Jade Screen) raises a barrier that blocks enemy projectiles and, more importantly, buffs her charged attack by 12% for a few seconds whenever a gem passes through it. Her charged attack fires six Star Jades that home in and deal Geo damage. Her burst (Starshatter) detonates the screen for AoE Geo and generates seven Star Jades instantly, which is a huge on-demand nuke.\n\nBecause her screen is the core, placement matters: cast it so her charged attacks pass through it for the damage bonus, then burst to convert it into a wave of projectiles. Her passive also makes her a strong explorer - she reveals ore on the map and takes less damage from falling rocks.\n\nBuild her like a normal Geo carry: ATK% Sands, Geo DMG Goblet, CRIT Circlet, with Energy Recharge around 115-130% for the 80-cost burst. 4pc Archaic Petra (stealing elemental resonances) is her strongest set, while 2pc Geo plus 2pc ATK is the safe fallback. Weapons favor stat sticks: Lost Prayer to the Sacred, Memory of Dust, Solar Pearl and Skyward Atlas all perform well.\n\nHer best teams are mono-Geo with Zhongli, Albedo, Gorou or Navia, and she shines in Geo resonance. Where she struggles: she is single-target, her screen can be destroyed by enemies, and she is weak against flying enemies her gems cannot reach."
  },
  "noelle": {
    "guide": "Noelle is the maid who does everything, and her ceiling depends entirely on constellation six. Her skill (Breastplate) grants a shield that scales with DEF and converts her normal attacks to Geo while it is active. Her burst (Sweeping Time) is the payoff: for 15 seconds her attacks gain a Geo infusion, her ATK scales with her DEF, and every hit heals the entire party. Without C6 her burst does not grant the Geo infusion, which is why pre-C6 Noelle is a healer-shielder, not a DPS.\n\nAt C6, build her as a true Geo carry. 4pc Husk of Opulent Dreams is mandatory because it stacks DEF and Geo DMG from her own skill, and her stats become DEF% Sands, Geo DMG Goblet, CRIT Circlet - unusual for a DPS but correct, since her burst converts DEF to ATK. Her fourth ascension passive also lets her heal and occasionally shield on hit during the burst. Weapons are Redhorn Stonethresher (signature-class), Serpent Spine or Whiteblind.\n\nHer teams are mono-Geo: Gorou for the DEF and Geo buffs, Albedo for off-field damage, Zhongli for shielding and resistance shred. Where she struggles before C6: her damage is low and her burst is expensive (80 cost), so she needs heavy Energy Recharge and a battery. At C6 she becomes a genuinely strong, self-sufficient carry."
  },
  "diona": {
    "guide": "Diona is the Cryo bow who splits her value between shielding and healing - and she is the budget version of every Cryo support rolled into one. Her skill (Icy Paws) fires shielding cat paws: tap creates two, hold creates five, and each also throws Cryo energy at enemies. Her burst (Signature Mix) creates a Drunken Mist field that heals the active character and grants a Cryo DMG Bonus while slowing enemies, acting as both sustain and a mini-ttds for Cryo carries.\n\nHer build depends on the role. For shielding, stack HP% because her shield scales with Max HP, and use Tenacity of the Millelith to add an ATK buff on top. For energy, Favonius Warbow is excellent because her paws generate particles and the weapon refund lets her battery the team. 2pc Maiden plus 2pc Tenacity is the common healer setup; Noblesse Oblige works if she is the burst buffer.\n\nHer best homes are Freeze teams (where her shield plus Cryo application is comfortable), Physical teams (Superconduct plus healing), and as a Cryo battery for Ayaka or Ganyu. Where she struggles: her shield, while good, is weaker than Zhongli's, her burst healing is modest, and her personal damage is negligible. She competes directly with Layla and Shenhe depending on what the team needs."
  },
  "yun-jin": {
    "guide": "Yun Jin is a Geo polearm support whose entire purpose is buffing normal attacks. Her burst (Cliffbreaker's Banner) grants Flying Cloud Flag Formation to nearby party members, increasing their normal attack damage by an amount that scales with her own DEF. That makes her a direct buffer for any carry who lives on normal attacks - Yoimiya, Itto, and Physical units all benefit disproportionately.\n\nHer skill (Opening Flourish) creates a shield that also scales with DEF, so her whole build funnels into one stat. Her passive further rewards DEF by adding a flat normal attack damage bonus when her shield is active, and Geo resonance plus a second Geo unit extends her buff duration. Unlike most buffers, she does not care about the carry's element - only that they auto-attack.\n\nBuild her full DEF%: DEF% Sands, DEF% Goblet, DEF% Circlet if you can keep CRIT for her tiny personal damage, otherwise DEF% everywhere, with Energy Recharge around 180-220% because her burst is 80 cost and her personal particle generation is low. 4pc Husk of Opulent Dreams is her best set for obvious reasons. Staff of Homa is the premium weapon, with Favonius Lance and Engulfing Lightning as strong alternates.\n\nWhere she struggles: her buff only helps normal-attack damage, so she is useless for burst or skill-focused carries, and her own energy needs are demanding. She is a niche but powerful pick for the right auto-attacker."
  },
  "sayu": {
    "guide": "Sayu is the Anemo claymore whose whole kit is about rolling and healing - a combination no other unit offers. Her skill (Yoohoo Art: Fuuin Dash) lets her roll on press for a short tumble or hold to roll longer, dealing Anemo damage and, if she picks up an element mid-roll, gaining that element for a Swirl on release. Her burst (Yoohoo Art: Mujina Flurry) deals AoE Anemo and summons the Muji-Muji Daruma, which either heals low-HP characters or attacks enemies depending on team HP - making her a rare off-field healer.\n\nHer role is flexible: build her for Swirl damage with Elemental Mastery and Viridescent Venerer, or lean into the healing with HP and a Clam set. Because she is Anemo, she can hold VV and still contribute healing through her burst, which is a tidy two-in-one. Energy Recharge matters since her burst is her main sustain tool - typically 180-220%. Weapons favor Favonius Greatsword for the particles, or Sacrificial Greatsword to reset her skill.\n\nHer best teams are Swirl comps and any team that wants a self-sufficient Anemo healer, including Sucrose-style driver setups and exploration parties where her roll is also a fast travel tool. Where she struggles: her damage is low even for an Anemo unit, her healing is spread thin rather than bursty, and her roll can be awkward to control. She is a comfort and utility pick, not a ceiling unit."
  },
  "mika": {
    "guide": "Mika is a Cryo polearm support built around two things: healing and attack-speed buffing for Physical carries. His skill (Flowfrost Arrow) has a tap form that creates a Flowfrost Arrow following the active character and dealing Cryo, and a hold form that deals more and grants його first ascension buff - Skyfeather Song - which raises the active character's Physical DMG based on Mika's ATK and heals them. His burst (Skyfeather Sonnet) heals the team and grants Reluctant Benison, speeding up normal attack speed, with his C6 adding a large ATK SPD and Physical boost.\n\nThat makes him the dedicated enabler for Physical DPS units - Eula, Freminet, Razor - where the attack-speed buff directly increases rotation damage. His buff scales with his ATK, so a meaningful chunk of his build goes to ATK% Sands and an ATK weapon, while HP and Energy Recharge keep his healing and burst online.\n\nFor sets, 4pc Noblesse Oblige supports the team, Song of Days Past amplifies his healing, and Ocean-Hued Clam turns that healing into damage. Favonius Lance is the safest weapon for his own energy, with Skyward Spine as an alternative. Energy needs are high - around 200-240% - because his 80-cost burst is his main value.\n\nWhere he struggles: he is locked to Physical teams, his buffs do nothing for non-Physical carries, and his personal damage is negligible. Outside Eula and Freminet he has little reason to be on the team."
  },
  "qiqi": {
    "guide": "Qiqi is the poster child for over-healing: her numbers are enormous, but her kit is slow and clunky. Her skill (Herald of Frost) applies Cryo, heals the active character, and marks enemies with the Fortune-Preserving Talisman, so that any hit on those enemies heals the attacker. Her burst (Preserver of Fortune) is a large AoE heal plus a party-wide version of the talisman mark, making her one of the strongest raw healers in the game.\n\nThe tradeoff is tempo. Her skill has a long cooldown and her burst is 80 cost, so she needs heavy Energy Recharge - often 200% or more - and her healing only really kicks in after you have marked enemies, which delays it. Build her for HP% and Healing Bonus, with a Healing Bonus circlet; 2pc Maiden Beloved plus 2pc Tenacity of the Millelith is standard, and Ocean-Hued Clam gives her some offense. Aquila Favonia is her premium weapon for the ATK and healing, with Sacrificial Sword or Favonius Sword for energy.\n\nHer best homes are teams that want a bulletproof Cryo enabler and sustain, including Superconduct Physical teams and Freeze. Where she struggles: her damage is essentially zero, her application is slow, and modern healers like Charlotte or Yaoyao do her job with far better uptime. She remains a comfort pick for players who want to never die."
  },
  "razor": {
    "guide": "Razor is the wolf boy, a Physical claymore carry whose kit is built entirely around his Electro-infused burst mode. His skill (Claw and Thunder) on press deals Electro and generates particles; on hold (Lightning Fang) he summons the Wolf Within, which boosts his Electro, reduces nearby enemy DEF, and gives him energy. His burst (Lightning Fang) flips him into an Electro state that greatly increases his attack speed and interruption resistance - this is his damage window.\n\nBecause his field buffs Electro and shreds DEF, he pairs naturally with Electro batteries like Fischl and Beidou, plus a Cryo unit for Superconduct to cut Physical RES, and Bennett for ATK. His fourth ascension passive reduces his skill cooldown when he takes damage, which rewards aggressive play.\n\nBuild him for Physical: ATK% Sands, Physical DMG Goblet, CRIT Circlet, with 4pc Pale Flame for skill-scaling or 2pc Pale Flame plus 2pc Bloodstained Chivalry. Energy needs are moderate because his burst is self-sustaining. Wolf's Gravestone is his best weapon, with Serpent Spine, Song of Broken Pines and R5 Snow-Tombed Starsilver as strong budget picks.\n\nWhere he struggles: he is a selfish on-field carry with no off-field value, his Electro state can interfere with reactions if you are not careful, and he is outclassed by newer Physical units like Eula and Freminet at high investment. As a free starter DPS, though, he remains a solid farmer."
  },
  "amber": {
    "guide": "Amber is the free Pyro bow whose reputation underestimates her utility. Her skill (Explosive Puppet) drops Baron Bunny, a taunt that draws enemy fire and explodes for AoE Pyro - a reliable off-field Pyro application and crowd control tool. Her burst (Fiery Rain) rains Pyro in a small area for several seconds, giving her genuine off-field Pyro uptime that few other bow users match.\n\nHer biggest hidden value is constellation six: it makes her charged attacks apply Pyro to enemies, turning her into a Vaporize enabler for carry units like Hu Tao or Diluc. Build her for EM in that role to amplify the reactions, or for ATK if you want her own burst damage. 4pc Wanderer's Troupe rewards her charged attacks, while 2pc ATK plus 2pc EM is the budget mix. Weapons favor Hamayumi, Sharpshooter's Oath, or Elegy for the End and The Stringless for support.\n\nHer best teams are as a Pyro applier in Vaporize comps, a C6 enabler, and an exploration pick thanks to her gliding passive. Where she struggles: her personal damage is low, Baron Bunny has a long cooldown, and she is easily outclassed by every other Pyro unit in pure DPS. She is a toolkit character - useful precisely because she is free and flexible."
  },
  "lisa": {
    "guide": "Lisa is the free Electro catalyst whose kit is built around stacking Conductive and bursting. Her skill (Violet Arc) on press deals a single hit and applies one stack of Conductive; on hold it consumes all stacks for a large AoE discharge. Her burst (Catharsis) creates a field that continuously applies Conductive and deals Electro, and her passive reduces enemy DEF whenever she hits a Conductive target - making her a budget DEF shredder.\n\nBecause her burst is a long-duration field, she works as an off-field Electro applier in Electro-Charged and Aggravate teams. Build her for Elemental Mastery in Aggravate (where her field keeps enemies infused) or ATK for general use. 4pc Golden Troupe boosts her skill-based damage, while 2pc Electro plus 2pc ATK is the simple route. Hakushin Ring is a strong support weapon that buffs the team's Electro or Dendro, with Mappa Mare and The Widsith as alternatives.\n\nHer best homes are Aggravate cores with Nahida and a Quicken trigger, and Electro-Charged teams. Where she struggles: her hold-skill is clunky and easy to mistime, her application is single-target, and her DEF shred only triggers on Conductive hits. She is a serviceable free unit but rarely a ceiling pick."
  },
  "thoma": {
    "guide": "Thoma is the Pyro polearm shielder whose value compounds the longer a fight runs. His skill (Blazing Blessing) grants a shield that follows the active character and deals Pyro damage, and his burst (Crimson Ooyoroi) creates a larger shield plus Fiery Collapse - coordinated attacks that deal Pyro whenever the active character's normal attacks land. His shields stack rather than refresh, so his uptime improves the longer he stays on the team.\n\nTwo passives define his build. His shield strength scales with Max HP, so HP% is his core stat, and his A4 grants a normal attack DMG bonus to the active character while his shield is up - turning him into a quiet attacker-speed buffer. That makes him a natural partner for normal-attack carries and for Burgeon teams, where his Pyro application feeds Bloom cores.\n\nFor sets, 4pc Emblem of Severed Fate keeps his burst up (he wants around 180-220% ER), while 2pc Tenacity plus 2pc Emblem is the support mix. Favonius Lance is the best weapon for his energy and the team's, with Staff of Homa and Engulfing Lightning as premium options.\n\nHis best teams are Burgeon (with Nilou or a Dendro core), Vaporize, and any comp wanting a Pyro shield. Where he struggles: his personal damage is low, his shields are weaker than Zhongli's, and he needs significant ER investment to feel smooth."
  },
  "heizou": {
    "guide": "Heizou is the only Anemo catalyst on-field DPS, and his damage comes from stacking Declension. His passive grants Declension stacks whenever a Swirl happens near him or when his skill hits - up to four - and his skill (Heartstring Noodles) consumes those stacks for a stronger, larger Anemo strike. His burst (Fudou Style: Windwalker Kick) deals AoE Anemo and refreshes the field. At four stacks his skill becomes a big nuke, so the loop is simple: Swirl to build stacks, then skill to cash them in.\n\nBecause he is Anemo and wants to Swirl, build him for the reaction: Elemental Mastery with 4pc Viridescent Venerer, or 4pc Desert Pavilion for raw Anemo damage. ATK% Sands, Anemo DMG Goblet and CRIT Circlet are standard, with modest Energy Recharge since his burst is a bonus rather than his core. Lost Prayer, Kagura's Verity and The Widsith are his best weapons, with Solar Pearl or Mappa Mare as free options.\n\nHis best teams are Swirl DPS, Aggravate with Nahida, and Quicken comps where he both triggers and benefits from the reaction. Where he struggles: he is a melee catalyst with short range, his damage falls off against enemies that resist Swirl, and he has no shielding or healing. He is a fun, self-sufficient hypercarry for Anemo fans."
  },
  "candace": {
    "guide": "Candace is the Hydro polearm whose claim to fame is normal-attack infusion. Her skill (Sacred Rite: Heron's Sanctum) on hold creates a shield and deals Hydro, and her passive makes the shielded active character's attacks deal Hydro. Her burst (Sacred Rite: Wagtail's Tide) grants Prayer of the Crimson Crown, infusing the active character's normal attacks with Hydro and boosting their ATK based on Candace's Max HP - a rare normal-attack buff that also changes the element of the carry's hits.\n\nThat makes her a specialist for normal-attack carries who want Hydro application without swapping off-field - Yoimiya, Itto, and even Ayato benefit. Build her for HP% and Energy Recharge, because both her shield and her ATK buff scale with HP and her 80-cost burst needs to be up often. 4pc Emblem of Severed Fate is the standard for burst uptime, with Tenacity of the Millelith as a support option. Favonius Lance is the safest weapon, with Staff of Homa or Key of Khaj-Nisut as premium picks.\n\nWhere she struggles: her infusion only affects normal attacks, so burst or skill-focused carries gain nothing, and her personal damage is negligible. She is a niche enabler whose value is entirely in the right auto-attack comp."
  },
  "chongyun": {
    "guide": "Chongyun is the Cryo claymore whose skill turns melee characters into Cryo units - a unique enabler role. His skill (Spirit Blade: Chonghua's Layered Frost) creates a field that infuses the normal attacks of sword, claymore and polearm characters with Cryo, letting Pyro carries like Hu Tao, Diluc or Ganyu trigger Melt on every hit. His burst (Spirit Blade: Cloud-Parting Star) deals AoE Cryo and creates a second field that slows enemies and applies Cryo.\n\nThat infusion is his whole identity. As a support he wants 4pc Noblesse Oblige to buff the team; as a DPS he runs 4pc Blizzard Strayer for Freeze. Stats are ATK% Sands, Cryo DMG Goblet, CRIT Circlet, with Energy Recharge around 150-180% for his 80-cost burst. Wolf's Gravestone and Serpent Spine are his best weapons, with R5 Lithic Blade and Sacrificial Greatsword as budget options.\n\nHis best teams are Melt comps where he enables a Pyro carry, Superconduct Physical teams, and Freeze cores. Where he struggles: his infusion can clash with other elements (you generally cannot run him alongside another melee Cryo infuser), his field has a radius limit, and his own damage is modest compared to dedicated Cryo DPS. He is a specialist tool, excellent when his infusion is exactly what the team needs."
  },
  "xinyan": {
    "guide": "Xinyan is the rock-and-roll Pyro claymore who combines shielding with Physical damage. Her skill (Sweeping Fervor) grants a shield that scales with DEF and deals Pyro, and the number of enemies hit determines her Riff stacks: zero gives an ATK bonus, one adds Physical DMG, and two or more also shreds enemy Physical RES. Her burst (Riff Revolution) deals AoE Pyro and applies Pyro to nearby enemies.\n\nHer fourth ascension passive grants a Physical DMG Bonus to shielded characters, and her C4 lowers enemy Physical RES further, so she both shields and amplifies a Physical carry. Build her either as a Physical DPS with 4pc Pale Flame (or 2pc Pale Flame plus 2pc Bloodstained) and ATK%/Physical/CRIT, or as a support with 4pc Tenacity of the Millelith. Serpent Spine and Wolf's Gravestone are her best weapons, with R5 Debate Club as a strong free pick.\n\nHer best teams are Physical cores alongside Rosaria or Fischl for Superconduct, and she fits in comfort comps that want a shield plus RES shred. Where she struggles: her shielding is mediocre, her Riff stacks require hitting multiple enemies to reach the RES shred, and she is outclassed by dedicated Physical units. She is a fun hybrid with a clear niche at C4."
  },
  "yanfei": {
    "guide": "Yanfei is the Pyro catalyst law-enforcer whose damage rides on Scarlet Seals. She accumulates up to three seals from normal attacks, her skill, and her burst; her charged attack (Seal Slash) consumes them for bonus damage and applies Pyro. Her skill (Signed Edict) grants a shield and adds a seal, while her burst (Done Deal) deals AoE Pyro, grants max seals and extends their duration - making her a sustained charged-attack DPS.\n\nBecause her charged attacks are her core, build her like a catalyst charged-attack carry: 4pc Wanderer's Troupe is her best set, with 4pc Crimson Witch for Vaporize. ATK% Sands, Pyro DMG Goblet, CRIT Circlet, and Energy Recharge around 115-135% for her 80-cost burst. Lost Prayer, Skyward Atlas and Solar Pearl are her best weapons, with Memory of Dust and A Thousand Floating Dreams as alternatives.\n\nHer best teams are Vaporize with Xingqiu or Yelan, Mono Pyro, and Overload. Where she struggles: she is single-target, her charged attacks are slow and stamina-hungry, and she has no shielding or healing. She is a strong 4-star Pyro DPS but competes with Hu Tao and Lyney for the same role."
  },
  "kujou-sara": {
    "guide": "Kujou Sara is the Electro bow support whose value is an ATK buff that scales with her own ATK. Her skill (Tengu Stormcall) on tap fires a crow strike; on hold it creates a Crowfeather Cover that, when the active character's attack lands on it, grants an ATK bonus based on Sara's ATK stat. Her burst (Subzero Barrage) deals AoE Electro and leaves a Crowfeather that buffs the next attacker - effectively a team-wide ATK boost for Electro carries.\n\nBecause her buff scales with ATK, she wants ATK% Sands and an ATK weapon, plus enough Energy Recharge (around 180-220%) to keep her 80-cost burst up. 4pc Emblem of Severed Fate is the standard for burst uptime, with 2pc ATK plus 2pc Emblem as a fallback. Skyward Harp, Hamayumi and Mouun's Moon are her best weapons, with Elegy for the End and Favonius Warbow as support options.\n\nHer best teams are Electro carry buffers - Raiden, Keqing, Cyno, and even Xiao to a degree - where the ATK buff directly raises their damage. Where she struggles: her buff only helps the active character who triggers the Crowfeather, her application is single-target, and at C0 her ER needs are demanding. She is a dedicated Electro support with no flex value."
  },
  "collei": {
    "guide": "Collei is the free Dendro bow sub-DPS and the budget version of every Dendro enabler. Her skill (Floral Brush) throws a boomerang that deals Dendro on the way out and back, and her burst (Trump-Card Kitty) creates a Cuilein-Anbar zone that applies Dendro periodically for its duration. Her fourth ascension passive refunds energy when her skill hits, smoothing her rotations.\n\nBecause she is a pure enabler, build her for Elemental Mastery and either Dendro DMG or Energy Recharge. 4pc Deepwood Memories is her best set since it shreds Dendro RES for the whole team, while 2pc EM plus 2pc Dendro is the budget route. Elegy for the End is her premium support weapon, with The Stringless and Favonius Warbow as strong options.\n\nHer best teams are Dendro cores: Quicken and Spread with Tighnari, Nahida or Alhaitham, and Bloom variants. Where she struggles: her personal damage is low, her application has gaps between skill and burst, and she is outclassed by Nahida in sustained Dendro uptime. As a free unit, though, she is the easiest way to enable Dendro reactions early on."
  },
  "sigewinne": {
    "guide": "Sigewinne is the Hydro bow healer built around the Bond of Life mechanic. Her kit heals the team and, through her burst, grants an ATK bonus to all party members that scales with her Max HP and the amount of Bond of Life active - which is why she pairs so naturally with Furina, whose entire kit generates Bond of Life. Her skill fires healing bolts, and her A4 grants a Hydro DMG Bonus to the team based on her stats.\n\nBuild her for HP% and Healing Bonus, with a Healing Bonus circlet and enough Energy Recharge to keep her burst available. 4pc Tenacity of the Millelith is the standard support set, while Song of Days Past or Ocean-Hued Clam convert her healing into value. Aqua Simulacra is her best weapon, with Favonius Warbow, Sacrificial Bow and even Recurve Bow (for HP) as alternatives.\n\nHer best home is Furina teams, where she both heals the HP drain and amplifies the ATK buff Furina provides - a rare case of two units designed to enable each other. Where she struggles: her personal damage is negligible, her kit is almost entirely about healing and buffing, and outside Bond-of-Life teams her ATK bonus has little to scale from. She is a specialized premium healer."
  },
  "sethos": {
    "guide": "Sethos is the Electro bow on-field DPS who plays like a catalyst carry. After his skill (Secret Rite: Twilight Silt), his charged attack becomes a powerful beam-like shot (the Psiset) that costs no stamina and deals heavy Electro - and his fourth ascension passive speeds up his normal attacks after using his skill, letting him weave autos into the rotation. His burst boosts his charged-attack CRIT DMG, making the loop skill, normal attacks, then charged beam.\n\nBecause he wants to Aggravate, build him for Elemental Mastery and Electro: 4pc Gilded Dreams or 4pc Wanderer's Troupe, with ATK%/Electro/CRIT or an EM focus. Energy Recharge around 120-140% is enough since his burst is a bonus. Hunter's Path is his signature, but R5 Slingshot is a famously strong free pick thanks to its ER and damage substats, with The Stringless and Polar Star as alternatives.\n\nHis best teams are Aggravate with Nahida and a Quicken partner, and he is a natural duo with Tighnari. Where he struggles: his damage window is gated behind his skill, his single-target focus is weak against crowds, and he needs Dendro to reach his ceiling. He is a solid free-to-play-friendly Electro carry."
  },
  "kachina": {
    "guide": "Kachina is the Natlan Geo polearm sub-DPS who fights through her contraption, Turbo Twirly. Her skill (Go, Pussycat!) summons the Twirly, which deals Geo damage and moves around the field; while it is active and she is off-field, her passive increases its damage and builds Nightsoul points (Mountain King's Dewgar), tying her into Natlan team mechanics. Her burst deals AoE Geo and refreshes the field.\n\nBecause she is a Nightsoul unit and a Geo applier, her best role is the Scroll holder in Natlan reaction teams or a flexible Geo unit elsewhere. Build her for Energy Recharge and Geo damage: 4pc Scroll of the Hero of Cinder City is her strongest set when the team's reactions match her Geo, while 2pc Geo plus 2pc ATK is the fallback. Favonius Lance and Deathmatch are her best weapons, with Blackcliff Pole as a free option.\n\nHer best teams are Natlan comps (Mavuika, Kinich, Varesa) where she provides Scroll buffs and Nightsoul synergy, and Geo resonance teams. Where she struggles: her personal damage is low, her contraption can miss fast enemies, and she offers no healing or shielding. She is a competent, accessible Natlan enabler."
  },
  "ororon": {
    "guide": "Ororon is the Natlan Electro bow sub-DPS built around Nightsoul mechanics. His skill (Night's Sling) summons Cirrus, a bat that deals off-field Electro, and his passive triggers a coordinated Electro attack whenever an ally triggers a Nightsoul Burst nearby - making him a passive damage dealer that rewards playing Natlan units. His burst extends his Electro application and buffs the team.\n\nBecause he is a Nightsoul unit, his best role is the Scroll holder in Natlan reaction teams, where his off-field Electro enables Vaporize, Overload or Electro-Charged while he buffs the team. Build him for Elemental Mastery and Electro: 4pc Scroll of the Hero of Cinder City is his strongest set, with 2pc Electro plus 2pc ATK as the fallback. Chain Breaker is his signature, with Favonius Warbow, The Stringless and Elegy for the End as alternatives.\n\nHis best teams are Natlan comps - Mavuika, Kinich, Varesa, and Chevreuse Overload - where his Electro and Scroll buffs multiply team damage. Where he struggles: outside Nightsoul teams his value drops, his personal damage is modest, and he has no shielding or healing. He is a focused Natlan enabler."
  },
  "lan-yan": {
    "guide": "Lan Yan is the Liyue Anemo catalyst whose signature is a swirling shield. Her skill (Lustrous Moonrise) creates a shield that absorbs all elemental types and, when struck, absorbs the element it was hit with to boost her Swirl damage - a unique defensive-Swirl hybrid. Her burst deals AoE Anemo and refreshes the field, and her fourth ascension passive increases shield strength and Swirl damage based on her Elemental Mastery.\n\nThat dual identity means she can be built two ways: as a Swirl driver with 4pc Viridescent Venerer and full Elemental Mastery, or as a shield support with 4pc Tenacity of the Millelith and HP. Weapons follow the role - Wandering Evenstar and Sacrificial Fragments for Swirl, Hakushin Ring or Mappa Mare as free options, with Lost Prayer as a stat stick.\n\nHer best teams are Swirl comps, Anemo-flex slots, and any team wanting a shield plus crowd control. Where she struggles: her shield, while versatile, is weaker than Zhongli's, her Swirl damage is modest for an Anemo unit, and she competes with Kazuha and Sucrose for the VV role. She is a flexible, shield-bearing Anemo option."
  },
  "mizuki": {
    "guide": "Mizuki is the Inazuma Anemo catalyst who fights in a drifting, wind-walking state. Her skill (Passionate Appeal) puts her into a hovering state where she moves freely and deals Anemo damage with her attacks, and her fourth ascension passive grants a team-wide Elemental Mastery buff after she uses her skill - making her a rare EM-boosting Anemo unit. Her burst deals AoE Anemo and extends the field.\n\nBecause she buffs EM and wants to Swirl, build her for Elemental Mastery: 4pc Viridescent Venerer is her best set, with 4pc Desert Pavilion as an alternative. ATK%/Anemo/CRIT or a full EM focus both work depending on whether you want her personal damage or pure support. Weapons favor Lost Prayer, Kagura's Verity and The Widsith, with Hakushin Ring and Mappa Mare as free picks.\n\nHer best teams are Swirl DPS, Aggravate and Quicken comps where the EM buff directly raises reaction damage, and she pairs well with Nahida. Where she struggles: her on-field drifting state can be awkward to control, her personal damage is modest, and she has no shielding or healing. She is a thematically unique EM-buffing Anemo."
  },
  "iansan": {
    "guide": "Iansan is the closest thing to a second Bennett, and the comparison is misleading in one important way: Bennett's buff is a circle you stand in, while Iansan's is a moving buff that decays unless your on-field carry keeps covering ground. Understanding that single condition is the difference between a top-tier buffer and a mediocre one.\n\nHer burst (The Three Principles of Power, 70 energy, 12s duration) summons the Kinetic Energy Scale, which follows the active character and converts a share of Iansan's own ATK into an ATK bonus for them. Below 42 Nightsoul points the bonus scales with how many points she has left; at 42 or more the Scale switches to its stronger state, where the conversion is a much larger flat percentage of her ATK. The Scale then does something no other buffer does: it logs how far the active character moves and refunds Nightsoul points every second based on that distance. Movement is her fuel.\n\nIn practice her loop is skill (Thunderbolt Rush, which restores 54 Nightsoul points and gives a free Swift Stormflight stomp off a normal attack) into burst, then swap. Her first ascension passive grants Precise Movement after that stomp lands, adding 20% ATK and extra Nightsoul restoration, so never skip the stomp. Her fourth ascension passive also heals the active character whenever Nightsoul Bursts occur nearby, which is small but real sustain.\n\nBuild her for ATK above everything else, because both tiers of her buff scale off her ATK stat. Energy Recharge comes next - a 70-cost burst with no particle generation during its uptime usually lands somewhere in the 160-180% range, less with a Favonius Lance in the party. Staff of Homa is the premium pick purely for the ATK it produces; Favonius Lance is the team-friendly one. For artifacts, Scroll of the Hero of Cinder City is excellent when the team's reactions line up with her Electro, and Noblesse Oblige is the safe fallback.\n\nHer best partners are carries that constantly reposition: Varesa, Mavuika, Kinich, Clorinde, Gaming, Xiao. Each of them generates distance as a side effect of their normal rotation, which keeps her Nightsoul gauge topped up and her buff in the strong state.\n\nWhere she struggles: carries that stand still or attack in place - Neuvillette channelling his beam, Yoimiya parked in her normal attack string, any turret-style gameplay - starve her Nightsoul gauge, drop her to the weaker conversion tier, and can end the Scale early. She also contributes almost no damage and only incidental healing, so in teams that cannot use an ATK buff she is a wasted slot."
  },
  "varesa": {
    "guide": "Varesa is a plunge-attack carry wearing a catalyst, and almost none of her damage comes from her normal attacks. Her whole kit is built to set up one very large plunge and then immediately set up the next one, and her ceiling depends on how cleanly you can repeat that loop.\n\nThe engine is Nightsoul and the Fiery Passion state. Her skill (Riding the Night-Rainbow, two charges) rushes forward, grants Nightsoul points, and gives a Follow-Up Strike that converts the next normal attack input into a stamina-free charged attack that launches her into the air. The plunge that follows restores a large chunk of Nightsoul points, and at maximum she enters Fiery Passion for up to 15s, where her normals, skill and plunge all gain stronger versions. Her burst (Guardian Vent) is both an AoE Electro hit and a shortcut into Fiery Passion. While Fiery Passion is active, a plunge briefly triggers Apex Drive, which lets her spend a discounted mini-burst on an even bigger plunge that still counts as Plunging Attack damage. The practical loop is skill, charged attack, plunge, then repeat and finish with the mini-burst.\n\nHer first ascension passive, Rainbow Crash, adds a flat ATK-scaled bonus to the shockwave of the plunge that follows her skill, and that bonus is several times larger while Fiery Passion is active. This is why her plunge, not her burst, is the number you build around: level her normal attack talent first, because it carries the plunge multipliers.\n\nFor artifacts, Long Night's Oath is the most complete set since plunge damage is nearly her entire kit, while Obsidian Codex's large CRIT Rate bonus after consuming Nightsoul points is competitive - but watch her CRIT Rate ascension and avoid overcapping. Standard main stats apply: ATK% sands, Electro DMG goblet, CRIT circlet. Her energy needs are low for a modern carry, roughly 120-140%, because the Fiery Passion loop generates much of what she needs and her mini-burst is discounted.\n\nHer strongest archetype is Overload: Chevreuse shreds Electro RES and buffs ATK, Iansan adds a large ATK buff that Varesa's constant plunging and dashing keeps fed, and a Pyro unit sustains the reactions. Furina plus Xianyun is the other premium direction, since Xianyun buffs plunge damage directly and Furina amplifies everything.\n\nWhere she struggles: her damage lands at fixed points, so mobile bosses and small hitboxes cause missed plunges, and unlike a melee carry she cannot adjust once the plunge starts. She is also AoE-leaning, which makes her noticeably weaker in single-target boss chambers than against grouped enemies. Her raw Electro multipliers are only average for a modern carry, so without Overload or Quicken support she falls behind."
  },
  "ifa": {
    "guide": "Ifa is an Anemo healer whose buff is unusually narrow: he converts the party's Nightsoul points into a bonus to Swirl and Electro-Charged damage, and he heals with shots that scale off Elemental Mastery. He is not a general-purpose Jean replacement - he is a specialist for Electro-Charged cores, and outside them most of his kit does nothing.\n\nHis skill (Airborne Disease Prevention) grants 80 Nightsoul points and puts him into a hovering state with Cacucu, during which his normal attacks become Tonicshots that heal the whole party and deal Anemo damage. The healing scales with Elemental Mastery, which is why every artifact main stat goes to EM. The important passive is Field Medic's Vision: while his Nightsoul's Blessing is active, he gains one stack of Rescue Essentials for every Nightsoul point held anywhere in the party, and those stacks raise the Swirl and Electro-Charged damage of nearby party members. This is the part most players miss - Natlan teammates (Ororon, Mavuika, Citlali, Xilonen) directly increase his buff, while a party with no Natlan units leaves him at his own baseline. His second passive adds a chunk of EM whenever a nearby party member triggers a Nightsoul Burst.\n\nHis burst (Compound Sedation Field) throws a sedative that detonates into a wind current that pulls enemies and objects inward, giving him genuine if modest grouping. If it strikes an enemy already affected by Hydro, Pyro, Cryo or Electro, it applies a Sedation Mark that detonates for AoE damage of that element.\n\nBuild him full EM on all three main stats, level him to 90 because reaction damage scales hard with character level, and give him an EM catalyst - Sunny Morning Sleep-In, A Thousand Floating Dreams or Mappa Mare. Viridescent Venerer is the default set since he is on-field triggering Swirls himself; Scroll of the Hero of Cinder City is the alternative in Natlan cores. His energy needs are moderate, typically 140-170%, because most of his value sits in his skill.\n\nHis best teams are Electro-Charged shells: Ororon or Fischl for off-field Electro, a Hydro applier such as Furina, Yelan, Xingqiu or Ayato, and an on-field driver. Ororon is the standout partner because he is Natlan-aligned, applies Electro off-field, and feeds Rescue Essentials.\n\nWhere he struggles: his healing arrives one slow projectile at a time and only while he is on-field, so every second he spends healing is a second your carry is not dealing damage. His Rescue Essentials buff applies to Swirl and Electro-Charged only, so in Freeze, Melt, Aggravate or mono-element teams he is a weak healer with a mediocre pull. He also has almost no personal damage, and his burst grouping is far weaker than Kazuha's or Venti's."
  },
  "dori": {
    "guide": "Dori's niche is unique and narrower than it sounds: her burst is the only ability in the game that heals an ally and refunds them energy at the same time. If your team does not need both from the same slot, you are usually better off with a different Electro unit.\n\nHer burst (Alcazarzaray's Exactitude, 80 energy, 20s cooldown, 12s duration) summons the Jinni, which connects to one nearby character. That character continuously regenerates HP scaled off Dori's Max HP, continuously regenerates Energy, and is affected by Electro, while the connector between the lamp and the character zaps any opponent it touches. Only one Jinni can exist at a time, so everything she offers goes to a single unit.\n\nHer passives are where her build gets interesting. Compound Interest refunds her own energy when her skill hits - 5 Energy for every 100% Energy Recharge she has, capped per cast - which means ER investment on Dori pays for itself twice, once by filling her burst and once through the refund. Her other passive shaves a second off her skill cooldown every few seconds whenever the connected character triggers an Electro reaction, so she works best next to a carry that is constantly reacting.\n\nBuild ER first: an ER Sands plus an ER weapon, generally landing somewhere in the 180-220% range unless she has a battery. After that, HP% and Healing Bonus, since her healing scales with Max HP. Noblesse Oblige is the default set, Ocean-Hued Clam turns her heavy healing into extra damage, and Tenacity of the Millelith works because her skill does hit opponents. Skyward Pride is listed for its ER substat, but Favonius Greatsword and Sacrificial Greatsword are usually worth more to the team, and Sacrificial in particular doubles her particle generation and her energy refund.\n\nShe fits best next to a single energy-hungry on-field carry that also wants sustain - Physical teams such as Eula, where Superconduct and the energy refund both matter, or as a budget source of Electro resonance and healing in one slot.\n\nWhere she struggles: her healing is single-target, so she cannot drive Furina or answer off-field chip damage, and Kuki Shinobu does the same job better in almost every team - off-field Electro application, party healing from her skill, and Hyperbloom triggering. Dori's burst costs 80 energy with no particle generation while it is active, the Electro she applies to the connected character can disrupt auras in Freeze or Quicken teams, and her own damage is negligible."
  },
  "freminet": {
    "guide": "Freminet is a Physical damage dealer wearing a Cryo Vision, and that mismatch defines both his build and his ceiling. His single largest hit - a Level 4 Shattering Pressure - is pure Physical damage, and he is also the only character in the game whose passive rewards triggering Shatter.\n\nHis skill (Pressurized Floe) opens the Pers Timer for 10s. During it, his normal attacks release frost waves that deal Cryo damage (counted as Elemental Skill damage) and raise Pers's Pressure Level. Pressing the skill again fires Shattering Pressure, which changes with the level you have built: level 0 is Cryo only, levels 1-3 are a mix of Cryo and Physical that scales with pressure, and level 4 spends everything on a Physical blast. At level 4 his normal attack is also replaced by Shattering Pressure until you jump to cancel it. The basic loop is therefore skill, four normal attacks to reach level 4, then skill again for the finisher.\n\nHis burst (Shadowhunter's Ambush) ties it together: it deals AoE Cryo, resets the skill cooldown, and puts him into Subnautical Hunter for 10s, granting interruption resistance, a 70% reduction to his skill cooldown, extra Pressure per normal attack, and frost waves at double damage. That window ends the moment he leaves the field, so it is a committed on-field burst phase.\n\nHis fourth ascension passive gives 40% more Shattering Pressure damage for 5s after he triggers Shatter, which is the clearest team-building instruction in his kit: he wants a Hydro unit so enemies can be Frozen and then shattered by his claymore, plus an Electro unit for Superconduct to cut Physical RES.\n\nFor artifacts, Pale Flame 4pc is the standard Physical build, with ATK% sands, Physical DMG goblet and CRIT circlet. Song of Broken Pines is his thematic best option because it gives Physical DMG Bonus along with a team ATK buff; Serpent Spine and Snow-Tombed Starsilver are the practical choices. His C1, which adds 15% CRIT Rate to Shattering Pressure, is his most valuable early constellation.\n\nWhere he struggles: his damage is split across Cryo and Physical, so no single buffer scales all of it - Shenhe only touches the Cryo half. His Shatter passive is dead against any enemy that cannot be Frozen, which includes most bosses, and his multipliers are dated for a modern on-field carry. His burst state ends on swap, he needs long field time, and he is outclassed by Eula as a Physical carry and by Ayaka or Wriothesley as a Cryo one."
  },
  "charlotte": {
    "guide": "Charlotte is a team-wide healer who happens to be Cryo, and that combination - not her damage - is why she has a slot. Party-wide healing is rare among 4-stars, and it is exactly what Furina teams need to convert HP drain into Fanfare stacks.\n\nHer burst (Still Photo: Comprehensive Confirmation) is the whole package. It costs 80 energy, delivers a large instant heal to every party member scaled off her ATK, and then creates a Newsflash Field that keeps healing the active character while ticking Cryo damage to enemies inside it. Because the healing scales with ATK rather than HP, she wants ATK% and Healing Bonus, not HP% - a common building mistake. Her skill applies a Snappy Silhouette mark (tap) that ticks Cryo damage for a few seconds, or a longer and stronger Focused Impression mark if you hold to the Finisher Frame, at the cost of a much longer skill cooldown. Those mark ticks are her only off-field Cryo application, and they are also what let her hold Tenacity of the Millelith with reliable uptime.\n\nHer fourth ascension passive shifts her stats with the roster: each Fontaine character in the party increases her Healing Bonus, while each non-Fontaine character increases her Cryo DMG Bonus instead. Since her personal damage is negligible anyway, the practical reading is that her healing is strongest in Fontaine-heavy teams and slightly weaker in mixed ones.\n\nEnergy is her real constraint. An 80-cost burst with modest particle generation usually needs somewhere in the 180-220% ER range at C0, which makes an ER Sands the default and Favonius Codex or Prototype Amber more valuable than raw ATK weapons - including Skyward Atlas, which is a strong ATK stat stick but solves none of her problems. Noblesse Oblige is the best all-round set, with Tenacity of the Millelith, Song of Days Past and Ocean-Hued Clam as alternatives.\n\nHer best homes are Furina teams as the team-wide sustain, and Freeze cores alongside Ayaka, Ganyu or Wriothesley, where she covers healing, Cryo resonance and battery duties in a single slot.\n\nWhere she struggles: all of her healing is behind an 80-energy burst, so if the burst is not ready the team has no sustain at all. Her Cryo application is too slow to serve as the aura for a Pyro Melt carry, and her hold skill's longer cooldown desyncs from her 20s burst. As a pure sustain unit she also competes with supports that heal and buff at the same time, so in teams that do not need healing she contributes almost nothing."
  },
  "lynette": {
    "guide": "Lynette is the budget Anemo slot, and her value has almost nothing to do with grouping. What she actually brings is an indestructible taunt that absorbs an element and fires it back, plus a team ATK buff that scales with how many elemental types you can fit into the party.\n\nHer burst (Magic Trick: Astonishing Shift, 70 energy, 18s cooldown, 12s duration) summons the Bogglecat Box, which taunts nearby enemies, ticks Anemo damage, and absorbs the first Hydro, Pyro, Cryo or Electro it comes into contact with - with Cryo taking priority over Pyro over Hydro over Electro. Once absorbed, it fires additional Vivid Shots of that element for the rest of its duration, and her fourth ascension passive grants her burst 15% more damage after the conversion happens. The box cannot be destroyed while it is active, which makes her taunt more reliable than most.\n\nHer first ascension passive is the reason her team building looks odd: within 10s of using her burst, the whole party gains 8%, 12%, 16% or 20% ATK depending on whether the party contains one, two, three or four elemental types. In a typical three-element core you get 16%, and it is not worth distorting a team just to reach 20%. Note that the buff lasts 10s while the box lasts 12s, so it often expires before the box does.\n\nHer skill (Enigmatic Feint) dashes on press and, on hold, marks an enemy with Shadowsign while letting you steer her movement. It heals her on hit and then drains HP over the following seconds, which conveniently lets her use HP-change gear such as Marechaussee Hunter. Her C1 adds a small vortex pull when the thrust hits a marked enemy, which is the closest she gets to crowd control.\n\nViridescent Venerer is her default set, and she typically wants an ER Sands, landing around 160-180% ER at C0. Favonius Sword is the safest weapon; Iron Sting is the choice if you want to lean on Swirl damage instead.\n\nWhere she struggles: she cannot group enemies, which is the single biggest reason Kazuha, Sucrose and Venti are preferred in the Spiral Abyss. Her taunt does nothing against bosses and enemies that ignore it, her personal damage stays low even with heavy investment, and her ATK buff is both small and short. In practice she is a Viridescent Venerer holder with a modest buff and no crowd control."
  },
  "gaming": {
    "guide": "Gaming is a plunge-attack Pyro carry who pays HP for damage and heals it back himself. That self-sustaining loop - not his multipliers - is what makes him playable without a healer, and it puts him closer in feel to Xiao than to Hu Tao.\n\nHis skill (Bestial Ascent) pounces forward and launches him into the air, after which his plunge becomes Charmed Cloudstrider: Pyro damage that cannot be overridden by any infusion, counted as Plunging Attack damage, and it costs him a fixed slice of his Max HP on landing without ever dropping him below 10%. His first ascension passive returns a little HP whenever that plunge connects, and his fourth ascension passive gives the plunge 20% more damage while he is above 50% HP and increased incoming healing while he is below it. The rule is simple: stay above half HP and let his own kit top him up.\n\nHis burst (Suanni's Gilded Dance, 60 energy, 15s cooldown) heals him for a large chunk of Max HP, enters Wushou Stance with interruption resistance, and sends out Man Chai, which smashes into the target for AoE Pyro damage. When Man Chai returns to him it resets his skill cooldown, so the burst is both damage and the enabler for another round of plunges.\n\nBecause he drains his own HP on every plunge, he triggers HP-fluctuation gear by himself: Marechaussee Hunter stacks without Furina, and with Furina he both feeds her Fanfare and keeps his HP swinging. Crimson Witch of Flames is the alternative for pure Vaporize. Run a Pyro DMG goblet and CRIT circlet; ATK% is the usual Sands, though an EM Sands is competitive in Vaporize teams because most of his damage comes from a few very large reacted plunges. His ER needs are moderate, roughly 140-160%, and his C4 refunds energy on plunge hits.\n\nHis best teams are Vaporize with Xingqiu or Yelan plus Bennett, with the fourth slot going to Kazuha, Sucrose or ideally Xianyun, who buffs plunge damage directly. Furina variants and Chevreuse Overload are the other directions. Serpent Spine and Beacon of the Reed Sea are his best claymores, with the caveat that Serpent Spine's stacks are harder to keep while he is self-damaging.\n\nWhere he struggles: Hu Tao still has a much higher single-target ceiling, and Gaming's plunges are AoE-oriented, so his boss damage falls behind. A plunge lands at a fixed point, so mobile enemies and knockbacks cause whiffs - and a missed plunge still costs him HP. He also applies a lot of Pyro at once, so with only one Hydro applier some plunges land on a Pyro aura and produce no reaction or the weaker reverse Vaporize."
  },
  "kirara": {
    "guide": "Kirara is the Dendro shielder, and the trade she makes against Zhongli is the whole reason to use her: she gives up universal RES shred and raw shield strength in exchange for Dendro application, Dendro resonance, and a shield that stacks on top of itself.\n\nHer skill (Meow-teor Kick) creates a Shield of Safe Transport whose absorption scales with her Max HP and absorbs Dendro damage at 250% effectiveness. The useful trick is that recasting before the old shield breaks adds its remaining absorption to the new one and resets the 12s duration, so refreshing early is strictly better than waiting. Holding the skill turns her into the Urgent Neko Parcel box for up to 10s: movement, climbing and jumping all improve at the cost of much higher climbing stamina drain, she rams enemies for Dendro damage, and the state ends in a stronger Flipclaw Strike. Her first ascension passive grants a stack of Reinforced Packaging for each enemy she rams, and each stack becomes an extra partial shield when the state ends.\n\nHer burst (Secret Art: Surprise Dispatch, 60 energy, 15s cooldown) hurls a package that explodes for AoE Dendro damage and scatters Cat Grass Cardamom mines that detonate on contact or after a delay. Her C4 is the constellation that changes her role: it lets the shielded active character trigger coordinated Dendro attacks, finally giving her reliable off-field Dendro application.\n\nBuild HP in every slot, because shield strength scales with Max HP and her fourth ascension passive adds a small amount of skill and burst damage per 1,000 HP. Aim for roughly 45,000-55,000 HP, with around 120-150% ER if you want the burst every rotation, which matters more for Dendro application than for damage. Deepwood Memories is the default set when she is the team's only Dendro unit, since it makes her the shredder and the shielder at once; 2pc Vourukasha's Glow plus 2pc Tenacity of the Millelith maximizes the shield, and Instructor works in reaction teams. Key of Khaj-Nisut is her best weapon because its HP% also converts into team Elemental Mastery, which is exactly what Bloom and Aggravate teams want. Favonius Sword and Sapwood Blade are the practical alternatives.\n\nShe fits Nilou Bloom, where the shield absorbs Bloom self-damage, and Quicken or Aggravate cores built around Alhaitham, Nahida, Keqing or Clorinde.\n\nWhere she struggles: against Zhongli she absorbs less, shreds nothing, and her 250% Dendro bonus is nearly irrelevant outside Dendro content. Her shield protects only the active character, her off-field Dendro application is locked behind C4, her burst has a long animation and its mines miss mobile enemies, and the box state costs both field time and stamina inside a rotation."
  },
  "yaoyao": {
    "guide": "Yaoyao's value is compression: she is simultaneously the Dendro applier and the healer, which lets a Dendro team run one fewer support. The price is that her best healing is locked behind field time.\n\nHer skill (Raphanus Sky Cluster, 15s cooldown) summons Yuegui: Throwing Mode, which lobs White Jade Radishes that explode for Dendro damage and heal based on her Max HP. The targeting logic matters more than most players realize: if every nearby character is above 70% HP, the radish is thrown at an enemy, so she applies Dendro; if anyone drops to 70% or below, it goes to whoever has the lowest HP percentage instead. A healthy team gets Dendro application and a damaged team gets healing, and you cannot have both at once. Up to two Yuegui can be active at a time.\n\nHer burst (Moonjade Descent, 80 energy, 20s cooldown) deals AoE Dendro damage and enters Adeptal Legacy, during which radishes heal the entire party, their damage counts as Burst damage, up to three additional Yuegui are summoned, and she gains movement speed and Dendro RES. The catch is that the state is short and ends the moment she leaves the field, so it functions as an on-field healing window rather than a passive aura. Her first ascension passive makes her throw extra radishes while sprinting, jumping or running during it, and her fourth ascension passive adds a small regeneration-over-time effect after each explosion.\n\nHealing scales with Max HP, so build HP% with a Healing Bonus circlet, plus enough ER to fund an 80-cost burst - although most of her value sits in her skill, so ER should not come at the cost of HP. Black Tassel gives the most HP, Favonius Lance solves her energy and the party's, and Staff of Homa is a fine stat stick. Deepwood Memories is her best set because she is usually the only Dendro unit and can carry the shred; Tenacity of the Millelith and Instructor are alternatives.\n\nShe fits Nilou Bloom, Quicken and Spread cores with Alhaitham, Nahida or Tighnari, and Aggravate teams with Keqing or Clorinde.\n\nWhere she struggles: Baizhu does her job better in almost every measurable way - his healing is entirely off-field, he brings a refreshing shield for interruption resistance, and he buffs Dendro reaction damage. Yaoyao gives up all three and trades them for being a 4-star who consolidates Dendro and healing into one slot. Her burst healing requires her to stand on field for several seconds, which is a real DPS loss in tight rotations, her skill healing arrives in slow radish bounces that cannot answer burst damage, and outside of Deepwood she brings no buffs at all."
  },
};
